egin{bmatrix}5 & -2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 6 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 5 & -4\\end{bmatrix}.$" "Calculate the determinant of the matrix\n$$\n\\displaystyle U=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 6 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 5 & -4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186323750 :question/problem "Let \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.$\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." "Let \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186323751 :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}-2x-5}{{x}^{3}-5{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{{x}^{2}-2x-5}{{x}^{3}-5{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186323756 :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{5{t}^{2}+20t+6}{{t}^{3}+2{t}^{2}+t}\\,\\text{d}t=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{5{t}^{2}+20t+6}{{t}^{3}+2{t}^{2}+t}\\,\\text{d}t=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186323758 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}+x-3}{({x}^{2}-2x+3)(x+1)}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{{x}^{2}+x-3}{({x}^{2}-2x+3)(x+1)}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186323759 :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{3{x}^{2}+9x-4}{(x-1)({x}^{2}+4x-1)}\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{3{x}^{2}+9x-4}{(x-1)({x}^{2}+4x-1)}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186323760 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}(3{sin}^{2}⁡x+7{cos}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}(3{sin}^{2}⁡x+7{cos}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186323761 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(5{sin}^{2}⁡x+11{cos}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(5{sin}^{2}⁡x+11{cos}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186323766 :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}}{(x-2)(x-3)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{{x}^{2}}{(x-2)(x-3)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186323768 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186323769 :question/problem "$|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 7 & 4 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 5 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}|=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle |\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 7 & 4 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 5 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}|=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186323773 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x(1-{tan}^{2}⁡x)}{1-{tan}^{4}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x(1-{tan}^{2}⁡x)}{1-{tan}^{4}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186323774 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x+3{csc}^{2}⁡x-3{cot}^{2}⁡x+4}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x+3{csc}^{2}⁡x-3{cot}^{2}⁡x+4}}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186323775 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{{cot}^{2}⁡x-1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x(1-{cot}^{4}⁡x)}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{{cot}^{2}⁡x-1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x(1-{cot}^{4}⁡x)}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186323781 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{2}{5}($\n{{field-1}} $)$" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{2}{5}(\n$$\n{{field-1}} $)$"] [:db/cas 17592186323781 :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the indefinite integral by  $I=\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x.$\n\nTo use integration by parts, let's suppose that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{-x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-{e}^{-x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({e}^{-x})\\,(-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))-\\int (-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))(-{e}^{-x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)-\\frac{1}{2}\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)-\\frac{1}{2}J.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we can use integration by parts to evaluate\n\n$$\nJ=\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo do this, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{-x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-{e}^{-x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J & =\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({e}^{-x})\\,(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))-\\int (\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))(-{e}^{-x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+\\frac{1}{2}I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our equation for $I,$ we solve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)-\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+\\frac{1}{2}I) \\\\ I & =-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)-\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)-\\frac{1}{4}I \\\\ \\frac{5}{4}I & =-{e}^{-x}(\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)) \\\\ I & =-\\frac{{e}^{-x}}{5}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x & =(-\\frac{{e}^{-x}}{5}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))){|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =(-\\frac{{e}^{-\\pi /2}}{5}(0-2))-(-\\frac{1}{5}(0+2)) \\\\  & =\\frac{2{e}^{-\\pi /2}}{5}+\\frac{2}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}(1+{e}^{-\\pi /2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's denote the indefinite integral by  $$\n\\displaystyle I=\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo use integration by parts, let's suppose that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{-x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-{e}^{-x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({e}^{-x})\\,(-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))-\\int (-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))(-{e}^{-x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)-\\frac{1}{2}\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)-\\frac{1}{2}J.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we can use integration by parts to evaluate\n\n$$\nJ=\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo do this, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{-x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-{e}^{-x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J & =\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({e}^{-x})\\,(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))-\\int (\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))(-{e}^{-x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+\\frac{1}{2}I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our equation for $I,$ we solve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)-\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+\\frac{1}{2}I) \\\\ I & =-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)-\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)-\\frac{1}{4}I \\\\ \\frac{5}{4}I & =-{e}^{-x}(\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)) \\\\ I & =-\\frac{{e}^{-x}}{5}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x & =(-\\frac{{e}^{-x}}{5}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))){|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =(-\\frac{{e}^{-\\pi /2}}{5}(0-2))-(-\\frac{1}{5}(0+2)) \\\\  & =\\frac{2{e}^{-\\pi /2}}{5}+\\frac{2}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}(1+{e}^{-\\pi /2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186323782 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{10}($\n{{field-1}} $)$" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{10}(\n$$\n{{field-1}} $)$"] [:db/cas 17592186323782 :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the indefinite integral by  $I=\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x.$\n\nTo use integration by parts, let's suppose that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{-x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-{e}^{-x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({e}^{-x})(\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x))-\\int (\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x))(-{e}^{-x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+\\frac{1}{3}\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+\\frac{1}{3}J.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we can use integration by parts to evaluate\n\n$$\nJ=\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nLet\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{-x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-{e}^{-x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J & =({e}^{-x})(-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))-\\int (-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))(-{e}^{-x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)-\\frac{1}{3}\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)-\\frac{1}{3}I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our equation for $I,$ we solve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+\\frac{1}{3}J \\\\ I & =\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+\\frac{1}{3}(-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)-\\frac{1}{3}I) \\\\ I & =\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)-\\frac{1}{9}I \\\\ 10I & ={e}^{-x}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)) \\\\ I & =\\frac{{e}^{-x}}{10}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x & =(\\frac{{e}^{-x}}{10}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))){|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =(\\frac{{e}^{-\\pi }}{10}(0-(-1)))-(\\frac{1}{10}(0-1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-\\pi }}{10}+\\frac{1}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}({e}^{-\\pi }+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's denote the indefinite integral by  $$\n\\displaystyle I=\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo use integration by parts, let's suppose that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{-x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-{e}^{-x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({e}^{-x})(\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x))-\\int (\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x))(-{e}^{-x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+\\frac{1}{3}\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+\\frac{1}{3}J.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we can use integration by parts to evaluate\n\n$$\nJ=\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nLet\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{-x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-{e}^{-x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J & =({e}^{-x})(-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))-\\int (-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))(-{e}^{-x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)-\\frac{1}{3}\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)-\\frac{1}{3}I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our equation for $I,$ we solve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+\\frac{1}{3}J \\\\ I & =\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+\\frac{1}{3}(-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)-\\frac{1}{3}I) \\\\ I & =\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)-\\frac{1}{9}I \\\\ 10I & ={e}^{-x}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)) \\\\ I & =\\frac{{e}^{-x}}{10}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x & =(\\frac{{e}^{-x}}{10}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))){|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =(\\frac{{e}^{-\\pi }}{10}(0-(-1)))-(\\frac{1}{10}(0-1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-\\pi }}{10}+\\frac{1}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}({e}^{-\\pi }+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186323783 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-1}^{0}12{e}^{-4x}\\,\\text{d}x=$  \n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-1}^{0}12{e}^{-4x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$  \n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186323787 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{{csc}^{4}⁡x-1}{