-10 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n| ${C}^{-1}=\\,\\,$ |  | $1$ | $0$ | {{field-1}} | $0$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $0$ | $0$ | {{field-2}} | $0$ |  |  |\n|  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} | $0$ | $0$ |  |  |\n|  | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $1$ |  |  |" "Find the inverse of the matrix\n$$\n\\displaystyle C=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -10 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n| ${C}^{-1}=\\,\\,$ |  | $1$ | $0$ | {{field-1}} | $0$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $0$ | $0$ | {{field-2}} | $0$ |  |  |\n|  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} | $0$ | $0$ |  |  |\n|  | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $1$ |  |  |"] [:db/cas 17592186323849 :question/problem "Find all real eigenvalues of the matrix\n$B=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -4 \\\\ 16 & 12\\end{bmatrix}.$" "Find all real eigenvalues of the matrix\n$$\n\\displaystyle B=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -4 \\\\ 16 & 12\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186323851 :question/problem "Find the characteristic equation of the matrix \n$D=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -5 \\\\ 4 & -7\\end{bmatrix}.$" "Find the characteristic equation of the matrix \n$$\n\\displaystyle D=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -5 \\\\ 4 & -7\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186323856 :question/problem "Find the characteristic equation of the matrix \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -7 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.$" "Find the characteristic equation of the matrix \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -7 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186323857 :question/problem "Find the characteristic equation of the matrix \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.$" "Find the characteristic equation of the matrix \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186323862 :question/problem "Given the matrix \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$, \nfind a diagonal matrix $D$ and an invertible matrix $P$ such that $D={P}^{-1}AP.$" "Given the matrix \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ \nfind a diagonal matrix $D$ and an invertible matrix $P$ such that $D={P}^{-1}AP.$"] [:db/cas 17592186323864 :question/problem "Determine if the matrix\n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$\nis diagonalizable or not, and if it is diagonalizable, find a corresponding diagonal matrix $D.$" "Determine if the matrix\n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nis diagonalizable or not, and if it is diagonalizable, find a corresponding diagonal matrix $D.$"] [:db/cas 17592186323871 :question/problem "Determine if $v=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$ is an eigenvector of the matrix \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.$\nIf so, then what is the corresponding eigenvalue?" "Determine if $v=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$ is an eigenvector of the matrix \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\nIf so, then what is the corresponding eigenvalue?"] [:db/cas 17592186323876 :question/problem "Determine if the matrix\n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$\nis diagonalizable or not, and if it is diagonalizable, find a corresponding diagonal matrix $D.$" "Determine if the matrix\n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nis diagonalizable or not, and if it is diagonalizable, find a corresponding diagonal matrix $D.$"] [:db/cas 17592186323904 :question/problem "Find the characteristic equation of the matrix \n$B=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.$" "Find the characteristic equation of the matrix \n$$\n\\displaystyle B=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186323909 :question/problem "Find the characteristic equation of the matrix \n$B=\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 10 & 0 \\\\ -5 & -7 & 0 \\\\ 6 & -9 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.$" "Find the characteristic equation of the matrix \n$$\n\\displaystyle B=\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 10 & 0 \\\\ -5 & -7 & 0 \\\\ 6 & -9 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186323910 :question/problem "Find the characteristic equation of the matrix \n$B=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -8 & -4 \\\\ 3 & 8 & 3 \\\\ -6 & -10 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.$" "Find the characteristic equation of the matrix \n$$\n\\displaystyle B=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -8 & -4 \\\\ 3 & 8 & 3 \\\\ -6 & -10 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186323911 :question/problem "Find the characteristic equation of the matrix \n$B=\\begin{bmatrix}-9 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -8 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.$" "Find the characteristic equation of the matrix \n$$\n\\displaystyle B=\\begin{bmatrix}-9 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -8 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186323915 :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =2.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}-2\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ -2{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ 0 &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNotice that its augmented matrix $M$ is already in row echelon form:\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{2}=\\frac{3}{2}{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this in the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =2{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3} \\\\  & =2(\\frac{3}{2}{x}_{3})+3{x}_{3} \\\\  & =6{x}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}6{x}_{3} \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{3}=2,$ we get the eigenvector\n$v=\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$" "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =2.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}-2\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ -2{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ 0 &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNotice that its augmented matrix $M$ is already in row echelon form:\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{2}=\\frac{3}{2}{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this in the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =2{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3} \\\\  & =2(\\frac{3}{2}{x}_{3})+3{x}_{3} \\\\  & =6{x}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}6{x}_{3} \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{3}=2,$ we get the eigenvector\n$$\n\\displaystyle v=\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186323923 :question/problem "Given the matrix \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$, \nfind a diagonal matrix $D$ and an invertible matrix $P$ such that $D={P}^{-1}AP.$" "Given the matrix \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ \nfind a diagonal matrix $D$ and an invertible matrix $P$ such that $D={P}^{-1}AP.$"] [:db/cas 17592186323926 :question/problem "Determine if the matrix \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 5 \\\\ -10 & -9\\end{bmatrix}$ \nis diagonalizable or not, and if it is diagonalizable, find a corresponding diagonal matrix $D.$" "Determine if the matrix \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 5 \\\\ -10 & -9\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ \nis diagonalizable or not, and if it is diagonalizable, find a corresponding diagonal matrix $D.$"] [:db/cas 17592186323929 :question/problem "Calculate ${\\int }_{1/2}^{1}{\\int }_{x}^{1/x}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}\\,dy\\,dx.$" "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1/2}^{1}{\\int }_{x}^{1/x}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}\\,dy\\,dx.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186323930 :question/problem "Calculate ${\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y}^{1}\\frac{1}{1+{y}^{2}}\\,dx\\,dy.$" "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y}^{1}\\frac{1}{1+{y}^{2}}\\,dx\\,dy.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186323931 :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation \n$T:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}5x-1y \\\\ -10x+2y\\end{bmatrix}$\nand the vectors \n${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix},{v}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}.$\nWhich of the given vectors belong to the kernel of $T?$" "Consider the linear transformation \n$$\n\\displaystyle T:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}5x-1y \\\\ -10x+2y\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nand the vectors \n$$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix},{v}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\nWhich of the given vectors belong to the kernel of $T?$"] [:db/cas 17592186323935 :question/problem "![](images/8d/8de0dad0436135ef40aa26666d10659f4041275a915de153b6b4b45312c1c52f.png)\n\nEvaluate ${∬}_{D}{x}^{2}y\\,dA,$ where $D$ is the region shown in the picture above." "![](images/8d/8de0dad0436135ef40aa26666d10659f4041275a915de153b6b4b45312c1c52f.png)\n\nEvaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}{x}^{2}y\\,dA,\n$$ where $D$ is the region shown in the picture above."] [:db/cas 17592186323943 :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $T:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}-9x-3y+3z \\\\ -12x-4y+4z \\\\ 6x+8y+10z\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find $\\,\\text{Ker}(T).$" "Consider the linear transformation $$\n\\displaystyle T:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}-9x-3y+3z \\\\ -12x-4y+4z \\\\ 6x+8y+10z\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$ Find $\\,\\text{Ker}(T).$"] [:db/cas 17592186323944 :question/problem "Find a basis of $\\,\\text{Ker}(T)$ given that $T:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}3y-6z-6x \\\\ -4y+x+z \\\\ 2y+8z+8x\\end{bmatrix}.$" "Find a basis of $\\,\\text{Ker}(T)$ given that $$\n\\displaystyle T:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}3y-6z-6x \\\\ -4y+x+z \\\\ 2y+8z+8x\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186323947 :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation \n$T:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}2x-4y+4z \\\\ -5x+10y-3z \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ \nand the vectors \n${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},{v}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\nWhich of the given vectors belong to $\\text{Ker}(T)?$" "Consider the linear transformation \n$$\n\\displaystyle T:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}2x-4y+4z \\\\ -5x+10y-3z \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ \nand the vectors \n$$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},{v}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\nWhich of the given vectors belong to $\\text{Ker}(T)?$"] [:db/cas 17592186323951 :question/problem "${\\int }_{1/2}^{1}{\\int }_{1/x}^{4x}y\\,dy\\,dx=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1/2}^{1}{\\int }_{1/x}^{4x}y\\,dy\\,dx=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186323953 :question/problem "Evaluate the double integral\n${∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{(1+x+y)^{2}}\\,dA,$\nwhere $D$ is the finite region enclosed by the line\n$y=1-x$ and the coordinate axes." "Evaluate the double integral\n$$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{(1+x+y)^{2}}\\,dA,\n$$\nwhere $D$ is the finite region enclosed by the line\n$y=1-x$ and the coordinate axes."] [:db/cas 17592186323961 :question/problem "Evaluate the repeated integral\n${\\int }_{-2}^{-1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}(1+{x}^{2})}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y.$" "Evaluate the repeated integral\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-2}^{-1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}(1+{x}^{2})}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186323964 :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ on the surface can be written as\n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\n$$\n\nwhere $⟨\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),-1⟩$ represents a normal vector to the tangent plane.\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-xy) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-y \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-xy) \\\\  & =-x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the normal vector to the tangent plane is \n\n$$\n⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-y,\\,-x,\\,-1⟩\n$$\n\nOur tangent plane should be perpendicular to $⟨8,2\\pi ,2⟩.$ So, the normal vector of that plane must be parallel to that vector. In other words, for some constant $c$, we must have\n\n$$\nc\\cdot ⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-y,\\,-x,\\,-1⟩=⟨8,2\\pi ,2⟩.\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding coordinates, we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2c\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-cy &=8 \\\\ -xc &=2\\pi \\\\ -c &=2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get $c=-2.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n-x(-2)=2\\pi \\,⟹\\,x=\\pi .\n$$\n\nAnd substituting $c=-2$ and $x=\\pi $ into the first equation, we have \n\n$$\n2(-2)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi -(-2)y=8\\,⟹\\,y=2.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=\\pi $ and $y=2$ into our function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-xy \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi -(\\pi )(2) \\\\  & =-2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent plane is perpendicular to the vector  $v$ at $(\\pi ,2,-2\\pi ).$" "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ on the surface can be written as\n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\n$$\n\nwhere $$\n\\displaystyle ⟨\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),-1⟩\n$$ represents a normal vector to the tangent plane.\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-xy) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-y \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-xy) \\\\  & =-x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the normal vector to the tangent plane is \n\n$$\n⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-y,\\,-x,\\,-1⟩\n$$\n\nOur tangent plane should be perpendicular to $⟨8,2\\pi ,2⟩.$ So, the normal vector of that plane must be parallel to that vector. In other words, for some constant $c$, we must have\n\n$$\nc\\cdot ⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-y,\\,-x,\\,-1⟩=⟨8,2\\pi ,2⟩.\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding coordinates, we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2c\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-cy &=8 \\\\ -xc &=2\\pi \\\\ -c &=2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get $c=-2.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n-x(-2)=2\\pi \\,⟹\\,x=\\pi .\n$$\n\nAnd substituting $c=-2$ and $x=\\pi $ into the first equation, we have \n\n$$\n2(-2)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi -(-2)y=8\\,⟹\\,y=2.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=\\pi $ and $y=2$ into our function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-xy \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi -(\\pi )(2) \\\\  & =-2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent plane is perpendicular to the vector  $v$ at $(\\pi ,2,-2\\pi ).$"] [:db/cas 17592186323965 :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ on the surface can be written as\n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\n$$\n\nwhere $⟨\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),-1⟩$ represents a normal vector to the tangent plane.\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4{x}^{2}y+x{y}^{3}-6) \\\\  & =8xy+{y}^{3} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4{x}^{2}y+x{y}^{3}-6) \\\\  & =4{x}^{2}+3x{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nHence, the normal vector to the tangent plane is \n\n$$\n⟨8xy+{y}^{3},\\,4{x}^{2}+3x{y}^{2},\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nNote that the tangent plane is horizontal if and only if the normal vector is vertical. This means that the $x$- and $y$-components of the vector must be $0:$\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} &=0 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}8xy+{y}^{3} &=0 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}+3x{y}^{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n \n\nFrom the first equation, we get $y=0$ or ${y}^{2}+8x=0.$ So, we have two cases.\n\n- If $y=0$, substituting this into the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n4{x}^{2}+3x(0)^{2}=0\\,⟹\\,x=0.\n$$\n\n- If ${y}^{2}=-8x$, substituting this into the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n4{x}^{2}+3x(-8x)^{2}=0\\,⟹\\,x=0.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=0$ back into ${y}^{2}=-8x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=-8(0)\\,⟹\\,y=0.\n$$\n\nSo, in both cases, we have $x=0$ and $y=0.$ Finally, substituting these values into our function, we get\n\n$$\nz=4(0)^{2}(0)+(0)(0)^{3}-6=-6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point where the tangent plane is horizontal is $(0,0,-6).$" "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ on the surface can be written as\n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\n$$\n\nwhere $$\n\\displaystyle ⟨\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),-1⟩\n$$ represents a normal vector to the tangent plane.\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4{x}^{2}y+x{y}^{3}-6) \\\\  & =8xy+{y}^{3} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4{x}^{2}y+x{y}^{3}-6) \\\\  & =4{x}^{2}+3x{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nHence, the normal vector to the tangent plane is \n\n$$\n⟨8xy+{y}^{3},\\,4{x}^{2}+3x{y}^{2},\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nNote that the tangent plane is horizontal if and only if the normal vector is vertical. This means that the $x$- and $y$-components of the vector must be $0:$\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} &=0 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}8xy+{y}^{3} &=0 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}+3x{y}^{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n \n\nFrom the first equation, we get $y=0$ or ${y}^{2}+8x=0.$ So, we have two cases.\n\n- If $y=0$, substituting this into the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n4{x}^{2}+3x(0)^{2}=0\\,⟹\\,x=0.\n$$\n\n- If ${y}^{2}=-8x$, substituting this into the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n4{x}^{2}+3x(-8x)^{2}=0\\,⟹\\,x=0.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=0$ back into ${y}^{2}=-8x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=-8(0)\\,⟹\\,y=0.\n$$\n\nSo, in both cases, we have $x=0$ and $y=0.$ Finally, substituting these values into our function, we get\n\n$$\nz=4(0)^{2}(0)+(0)(0)^{3}-6=-6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point where the tangent plane is horizontal is $(0,0,-6).$"] [:db/cas 17592186323967 :question/problem "${\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{y}^{2}}^{y}(1-xy)\\,dx\\,dy=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{y}^{2}}^{y}(1-xy)\\,dx\\,dy=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186323969 :question/problem "Given the transformation $T:\\begin{bmatrix}w \\\\ x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}w-x+2y-z \\\\ x-y-z \\\\ 4w-y+z \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},$ find its rank." "Given the transformation $$\n\\displaystyle T:\\begin{bmatrix}w \\\\ x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}w-x+2y-z \\\\ x-y-z \\\\ 4w-y+z \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ find its rank."] [:db/cas 17592186323974 :question/problem "Find the value of $|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 8\\end{aligned}|.$" "Find the value of $$\n\\displaystyle |\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 8\\end{aligned}|.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186323975 :question/problem "Find the value of $|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -6 & -4\\end{aligned}|.$" "Find the value of $$\n\\displaystyle |\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -6 & -4\\end{aligned}|.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186323976 :question/problem "${\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta =$" "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta =\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186326669 :question/problem "Find the value of $|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -4\\end{aligned}|.$" "Find the value of $$\n\\displaystyle |\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -4\\end{aligned}|.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186326670 :question/problem "Find the value of $|\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}|.$" "Find the value of $$\n\\displaystyle |\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}|.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186326672 :question/problem "Find the value of $|\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}|.$" "Find the value of $$\n\\displaystyle |\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}|.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186326678 :question/problem "If $v=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}$ is an eigenvector of \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -4 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =\\sqrt{3}\\text{i},$\nthen what is the value of $\\frac{b}{a}?$" "If $v=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}$ is an eigenvector of \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -4 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =\\sqrt{3}\\text{i},$\nthen what is the value of $\\frac{b}{a}?$"] [:db/cas 17592186326678 :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =\\sqrt{3}\\text{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -4 & -1\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{3}\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{3}\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1-\\sqrt{3}\\text{i} & 1 \\\\ -4 & -1-\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}(1-\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}){x}_{1}+{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ -4{x}_{1}+(-1-\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}){x}_{2} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables to be the free variable. For example, from the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=-(1-\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}){x}_{1}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ -(1-\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}){x}_{1}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{1}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{1}=-1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1-\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{1-\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}}{-1}=\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}-1.$" "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =\\sqrt{3}\\text{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -4 & -1\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{3}\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{3}\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1-\\sqrt{3}\\text{i} & 1 \\\\ -4 & -1-\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}(1-\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}){x}_{1}+{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ -4{x}_{1}+(-1-\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}){x}_{2} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables to be the free variable. For example, from the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=-(1-\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}){x}_{1}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ -(1-\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}){x}_{1}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{1}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{1}=-1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1-\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{1-\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}}{-1}=\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}-1.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186326683 :question/problem "Change the order of integration and evaluate the following double integral ${\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{3}}^{1}\\frac{3{x}^{5}}{\\sqrt{{x}^{6}+{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Change the order of integration and evaluate the following double integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{3}}^{1}\\frac{3{x}^{5}}{\\sqrt{{x}^{6}+{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186326684 :question/problem "Let ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}$ and ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{2} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$ be linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix\n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2 \\\\ -5 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.$\nGiven that ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2+3\\text{i}$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ find the value of $\\frac{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}}{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}}.$" "Let ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}$ and ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{2} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$ be linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix\n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2 \\\\ -5 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\nGiven that ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2+3\\text{i}$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ find the value of $\\frac{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}}{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186326727 :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{6}$ into ${R}^{6},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,\\left\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{6} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=6 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}\\right.$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement $\\text{Ker}(T)={0}$ is true. Since $T$ is a bijection, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{Ker}(T)={0}.$\n\n- Statement $\\text{Im}(T)={R}^{6}$ is true. Since $T$ is a bijection, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{Im}(T)={R}^{6}.$\n\n- Statement $\\text{rank}(T)=6$ is true. Since $T$ is a bijection, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{rank}(T)=6.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\text{Ker}(T)={0}$; $\\text{Im}(T)={R}^{6}$; $\\text{rank}(T)=6$." "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{6}$ into ${R}^{6},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $$\n\\displaystyle \\,⟺\\,\\left\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{6} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=6 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement $\\text{Ker}(T)={0}$ is true. Since $T$ is a bijection, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{Ker}(T)={0}.$\n\n- Statement $\\text{Im}(T)={R}^{6}$ is true. Since $T$ is a bijection, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{Im}(T)={R}^{6}.$\n\n- Statement $\\text{rank}(T)=6$ is true. Since $T$ is a bijection, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{rank}(T)=6.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\text{Ker}(T)={0}$; $\\text{Im}(T)={R}^{6}$; $\\text{rank}(T)=6$."] [:db/cas 17592186326728 :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{5}$ into ${R}^{5},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,\\left\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{5} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=5 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}\\right.$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement $\\text{rank}(T)=3$ is true. Since ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(T)$ we must have that $\\text{rank}(T)=3.$\n\n- Statement “$T$ is invertible” is false. According to the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 5,$ the transformation $T$ and the matrix $T$ are not invertible.\n\n- Statement $\\text{nullity}(T)>0$ is true. Since $T$ is not invertible, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0.$ In turn, this means that $\\text{nullity}(T)>0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\text{rank}(T)=3$; $\\text{nullity}(T)>0$ only." "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{5}$ into ${R}^{5},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $$\n\\displaystyle \\,⟺\\,\\left\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{5} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=5 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement $\\text{rank}(T)=3$ is true. Since ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(T)$ we must have that $\\text{rank}(T)=3.$\n\n- Statement “$T$ is invertible” is false. According to the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 5,$ the transformation $T$ and the matrix $T$ are not invertible.\n\n- Statement $\\text{nullity}(T)>0$ is true. Since $T$ is not invertible, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0.$ In turn, this means that $\\text{nullity}(T)>0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\text{rank}(T)=3$; $\\text{nullity}(T)>0$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186326734 :question/problem "![](images/44/446e61491ae43e610dc39e4b46b752668969540f7733bf7d9c945fa260210a77.png)\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y}^{1}\\sqrt{1-{z}^{2}}\\,dz\\,dy\\,dx.$ The region of integration is shown above.\n\n*Hint: Change the order of integration to $\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x.$*" "![](images/44/446e61491ae43e610dc39e4b46b752668969540f7733bf7d9c945fa260210a77.png)\n\nEvaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y}^{1}\\sqrt{1-{z}^{2}}\\,dz\\,dy\\,dx.\n$$ The region of integration is shown above.\n\n*Hint: Change the order of integration to $\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x.$*"] [:db/cas 17592186326746 :question/problem "Let ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}$ and ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{2} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$ be linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix\n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 3 \\\\ -15 & -5\\end{bmatrix}.$\nGiven that ${\\lambda }_{1}=1-3\\text{i}$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ find the value of $\\frac{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}}{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}}.$" "Let ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}$ and ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{2} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$ be linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix\n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 3 \\\\ -15 & -5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\nGiven that ${\\lambda }_{1}=1-3\\text{i}$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ find the value of $\\frac{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}}{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186326747 :question/problem "Let ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}$ and ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{2} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$ be linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix\n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}-8 & -4 \\\\ 20 & 8\\end{bmatrix}.$\nGiven that ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4\\text{i}$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ find the value of $\\frac{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}}{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}}.$" "Let ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}$ and ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{2} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$ be linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix\n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}-8 & -4 \\\\ 20 & 8\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\nGiven that ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4\\text{i}$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ find the value of $\\frac{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}}{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186326748 :question/problem "Given that ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}$ and ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{2} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$ are linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix\n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 10 \\\\ -5 & 12\\end{bmatrix},$\nwhat is the value of $\\frac{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}}{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}}?$" "Given that ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}$ and ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{2} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$ are linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix\n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 10 \\\\ -5 & 12\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\nwhat is the value of $\\frac{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}}{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}}?$"] [:db/cas 17592186326759 :question/problem "If the path $C$ is the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ traversed from the point $A(1,2)$ to the point $B(3,4),$ then ${\\int }_{C}x\\,dx+y\\,dy=$" "If the path $C$ is the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ traversed from the point $A(1,2)$ to the point $B(3,4),$ then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}x\\,dx+y\\,dy=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186326763 :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ given that $F(x,y)=(y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\,i+(x+y)\\,j$ and $C$ is the path  $r(t)=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡t\\,i+2j$ for $t\\in [0,\\frac{1}{2}].$" "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$ given that $F(x,y)=(y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\,i+(x+y)\\,j$ and $C$ is the path  $r(t)=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡t\\,i+2j$ for $t\\in [0,\\frac{1}{2}].$"] [:db/cas 17592186326764 :question/problem "![](images/c5/c5e026312ab8a2ccfc61a37246c954b56550316661f158743cc2c46411a97f53.png)\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{C}F(r)\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $F(x,y)=(y-x)\\,i-x\\,j$ and $C$ is the arc of the ellipse ${x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}=2$  traversed counterclockwise from the point $(\\sqrt{2},0)$ to the point $(0,1).$" "![](images/c5/c5e026312ab8a2ccfc61a37246c954b56550316661f158743cc2c46411a97f53.png)\n\nEvaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F(r)\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$ where $F(x,y)=(y-x)\\,i-x\\,j$ and $C$ is the arc of the ellipse ${x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}=2$  traversed counterclockwise from the point $(\\sqrt{2},0)$ to the point $(0,1).$"] [:db/cas 17592186326775 :question/problem "![](images/73/73c6a8268a179f72611fb65e099d1249d48501e4fd4731d03747800e1944fd97.png)\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ consists of a the line segment from the point $A(0,1)$ to the point $B(1,2),$ and the path ${C}_{2}$ consists of a line segment from the point $B(1,2)$ to the point $D(1,3).$\n\nGiven that ${\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=\\frac{7}{2},$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $F(x,y)=(2x-3y)\\,i+(x+y)\\,j,$ and $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}.$" "![](images/73/73c6a8268a179f72611fb65e099d1249d48501e4fd4731d03747800e1944fd97.png)\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ consists of a the line segment from the point $A(0,1)$ to the point $B(1,2),$ and the path ${C}_{2}$ consists of a line segment from the point $B(1,2)$ to the point $D(1,3).$\n\nGiven that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=\\frac{7}{2},\n$$ evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$ where $F(x,y)=(2x-3y)\\,i+(x+y)\\,j,$ and $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186326776 :question/problem "If the path $C$ is a part of the curve $y=\\sqrt[3]{x}$ traversed from the point $P(0,0)$ to point $Q(1,1),$ then ${\\int }_{C}4y{e}^{xy}\\,dx+dy=$" "If the path $C$ is a part of the curve $y=\\sqrt[3]{x}$ traversed from the point $P(0,0)$ to point $Q(1,1),$ then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}4y{e}^{xy}\\,dx+dy=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186326777 :question/problem "Find the general solution to the system of differential equations\n$\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=-3{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=\\pi {x}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\\right.$" "Find the general solution to the system of differential equations\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=-3{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=\\pi {x}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186326798 :question/worked-solution "A reciprocal function is defined for all values of $x$ except for those that make the denominator zero. So, to find the domain, we must exclude all values of $x$ that make the denominator zero.\n\nSetting the denominator equal to zero and solving, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-1 & =0 \\\\ 2x & =1 \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\frac{1}{2})\\cup (\\frac{1}{2},\\infty ).$" "A reciprocal function is defined for all values of $x$ except for those that make the denominator zero. So, to find the domain, we must exclude all values of $x$ that make the denominator zero.\n\nSetting the denominator equal to zero and solving, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-1 & =0 \\\\ 2x & =1 \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is $$\n\\displaystyle x\\in (-\\infty ,\\frac{1}{2})\\cup (\\frac{1}{2},\\infty ).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186326799 :question/worked-solution "A reciprocal function is defined for all values of $x$ except for those that make the denominator zero. So, to find the domain, we must exclude all values of $x$ that make the denominator zero.\n\nSetting the denominator equal to zero and solving, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-3 & =0 \\\\ 2x & =3 \\\\ x & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\frac{3}{2})\\cup (\\frac{3}{2},\\infty ).$" "A reciprocal function is defined for all values of $x$ except for those that make the denominator zero. So, to find the domain, we must exclude all values of $x$ that make the denominator zero.\n\nSetting the denominator equal to zero and solving, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-3 & =0 \\\\ 2x & =3 \\\\ x & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is $$\n\\displaystyle x\\in (-\\infty ,\\frac{3}{2})\\cup (\\frac{3}{2},\\infty ).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186326803 :question/worked-solution "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is ${x}^{2}.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by ${x}^{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{3}+2x+1}{{x}^{2}-x-12} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{12}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+\\frac{2}{x}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}{(1-\\frac{1}{x}-\\frac{12}{{x}^{2}})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $\\frac{2}{x},\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}},\\frac{1}{x}$ and $\\frac{12}{{x}^{2}}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(x+\\frac{2}{x}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}{(1-\\frac{1}{x}-\\frac{12}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{x+0+0}{1-0-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{1} \\\\  & =x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is not a constant, which means that $y=f(x)$ does not have a horizontal asymptote." "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is ${x}^{2}.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by ${x}^{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{3}+2x+1}{{x}^{2}-x-12} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{12}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+\\frac{2}{x}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}{(1-\\frac{1}{x}-\\frac{12}{{x}^{2}})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{2}{x},\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}},\\frac{1}{x}\n$$ and $\\frac{12}{{x}^{2}}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(x+\\frac{2}{x}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}{(1-\\frac{1}{x}-\\frac{12}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{x+0+0}{1-0-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{1} \\\\  & =x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is not a constant, which means that $y=f(x)$ does not have a horizontal asymptote."] [:db/cas 17592186326807 :question/problem "If $u=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\,,$ $v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}\\,,$ and $u\\cdot u=17,$ then $\\frac{u\\cdot (2u)}{u\\cdot (\\frac{1}{3}v)}=$" "If $u=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\,,$ $v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}\\,,$ and $u\\cdot u=17,$ then $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{u\\cdot (2u)}{u\\cdot (\\frac{1}{3}v)}=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186326816 :question/problem "${\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{z}{\\int }_{0}^{y+z}2xyz\\,dx\\,dy\\,dz=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{z}{\\int }_{0}^{y+z}2xyz\\,dx\\,dy\\,dz=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186326825 :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each series in turn.\n\n- For the geometric series $1+5+25+125+⋯$ the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{5}{1} \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is divergent because $|r|\\ge 1.$\n\n- For the geometric series $\\frac{2}{7}+\\frac{4}{3}+\\frac{56}{9}+\\frac{784}{27}+⋯$ the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{2}{7}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\cdot \\frac{7}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is divergent because $|r|\\ge 1.$\n\n- For the geometric series $\\begin{gathered}0.1+0.01+0.001+0.000\\,1+⋯\\end{gathered}$  the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{0.01}{0.1} \\\\  & =0.1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is convergent because $|r|<1.$\n\nIn conclusion, only series $\\begin{gathered}0.1+0.01+0.001+0.000\\,1+⋯\\end{gathered}$ converges." "Let's look at each series in turn.\n\n- For the geometric series $1+5+25+125+⋯$ the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{5}{1} \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is divergent because $|r|\\ge 1.$\n\n- For the geometric series $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{2}{7}+\\frac{4}{3}+\\frac{56}{9}+\\frac{784}{27}+⋯\n$$ the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{2}{7}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\cdot \\frac{7}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is divergent because $|r|\\ge 1.$\n\n- For the geometric series $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{gathered}0.1+0.01+0.001+0.000\\,1+⋯\\end{gathered}\n$$  the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{0.01}{0.1} \\\\  & =0.1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is convergent because $|r|<1.$\n\nIn conclusion, only series $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{gathered}0.1+0.01+0.001+0.000\\,1+⋯\\end{gathered}\n$$ converges."] [:db/cas 17592186326843 :question/problem "Find the cofactor of ${a}_{13}$ in \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -5 & 1 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & 1 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 7 & -2 & 5 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -5 & 1 & 1 & 6 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 & 4 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.$" "Find the cofactor of ${a}_{13}$ in \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -5 & 1 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & 1 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 7 & -2 & 5 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -5 & 1 & 1 & 6 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 & 4 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186326844 :question/problem "Find the standard matrix that  represents  the  linear  transformation $T:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{2},$ where\n$T:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}-3x-5y+2z \\\\ 2x-3y\\end{bmatrix}.$" "Find the standard matrix that  represents  the  linear  transformation $T:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{2},$ where\n$$\n\\displaystyle T:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}-3x-5y+2z \\\\ 2x-3y\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186326845 :question/problem "Find the scale factor of the transformation represented by the rotation-scaling matrix\n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}-2\\sqrt{5} & -4 \\\\ 4 & -2\\sqrt{5}\\end{bmatrix}.$" "Find the scale factor of the transformation represented by the rotation-scaling matrix\n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}-2\\sqrt{5} & -4 \\\\ 4 & -2\\sqrt{5}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186326846 :question/problem "${\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{z}{\\int }_{0}^{x+z}72xyz\\,dy\\,dx\\,dz=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{z}{\\int }_{0}^{x+z}72xyz\\,dy\\,dx\\,dz=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186326856 :question/problem "Which of the following statements is true regarding the series  ${\\sum }_{n=3}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{15}{\\left(\\frac{6}{5}\\right)}^{n-1}$ ?" "Which of the following statements is true regarding the series  $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=3}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{15}{\\left(\\frac{6}{5}\\right)}^{n-1}\n$$ ?"] [:db/cas 17592186326857 :question/problem "Consider the finite region $D$ in the $xy$-plane enclosed between the curves $y=x-2,$ and $y=\\sqrt{x},$ above the $x$-axis. What is ${∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA\\,$ expressed as a repeated integral?" "Consider the finite region $D$ in the $xy$-plane enclosed between the curves $y=x-2,$ and $y=\\sqrt{x},$ above the $x$-axis. What is $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA\\,\n$$ expressed as a repeated integral?"] [:db/cas 17592186326858 :question/problem "For which values of $k$ does the series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{16}^{kn+1}}{{4}^{n-1}}$ converge?" "For which values of $k$ does the series $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{16}^{kn+1}}{{4}^{n-1}}\n$$ converge?"] [:db/cas 17592186326858 :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the series so that it's in the form ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }a{r}^{n},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{16}^{kn+1}}{{4}^{n-1}} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{16}^{kn}\\cdot 16}{{4}^{n}\\cdot {4}^{-1}} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(16\\cdot 4){\\left(\\frac{{16}^{k}}{4}\\right)}^{n} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }64{\\left(\\frac{{16}^{k}}{4}\\right)}^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that this is a geometric series with a common ratio of $r=\\frac{{16}^{k}}{4}.$ \n\nFor the series to converge, we require $|r|<1.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\frac{{16}^{k}}{4}| & <1 \\\\ \\frac{{16}^{k}}{4} & <1 \\\\ {16}^{k} & <4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we could remove the absolute value bars because $\\frac{{16}^{k}}{4}$ is always positive.\n\nThe equation ${16}^{k}=4$ has the solution $k=\\frac{1}{2}.$ Now, since ${16}^{k}$ increases as $k$ increases, the solution to ${16}^{k}<4$ must be $k<\\frac{1}{2}.$" "First, we rewrite the series so that it's in the form $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }a{r}^{n},\n$$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{16}^{kn+1}}{{4}^{n-1}} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{16}^{kn}\\cdot 16}{{4}^{n}\\cdot {4}^{-1}} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(16\\cdot 4){\\left(\\frac{{16}^{k}}{4}\\right)}^{n} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }64{\\left(\\frac{{16}^{k}}{4}\\right)}^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that this is a geometric series with a common ratio of $r=\\frac{{16}^{k}}{4}.$ \n\nFor the series to converge, we require $|r|<1.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\frac{{16}^{k}}{4}| & <1 \\\\ \\frac{{16}^{k}}{4} & <1 \\\\ {16}^{k} & <4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we could remove the absolute value bars because $\\frac{{16}^{k}}{4}$ is always positive.\n\nThe equation ${16}^{k}=4$ has the solution $k=\\frac{1}{2}.$ Now, since ${16}^{k}$ increases as $k$ increases, the solution to ${16}^{k}<4$ must be $k<\\frac{1}{2}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186326871 :question/problem "![](images/a1/a135b0766239bcd6e9cacc324572486e66e1c859ecd28a4d59ef7795cc511ed4.png)\n\nEvaluate the double integral ${∬}_{D}xy\\,dA,\\,$\nwhere $D$ is the region enclosed between the curves ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4$ and $x=0,$ above the $x$-axis and below the line $y=1,$ as shown in the picture." "![](images/a1/a135b0766239bcd6e9cacc324572486e66e1c859ecd28a4d59ef7795cc511ed4.png)\n\nEvaluate the double integral $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}xy\\,dA,\\,\n$$\nwhere $D$ is the region enclosed between the curves ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4$ and $x=0,$ above the $x$-axis and below the line $y=1,$ as shown in the picture."] [:db/cas 17592186326873 :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{Col}(A)$ is the span of the columns of $A.$ \n\nFirst, let's denote\n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{(-3)\\cdot 2+4\\cdot (-1)+(-1)\\cdot 4}{{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{4}^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{2}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{5}{3} \\\\ \\frac{10}{3} \\\\ \\frac{5}{3}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B=\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{5}{3} \\\\ \\frac{10}{3} \\\\ \\frac{5}{3}\\end{bmatrix}\\}$ is an orthogonal basis for $\\text{Col}(A).$\n\nFinally, $b=\\frac{10}{3},$ $c=\\frac{5}{3},$ and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{b}{c}=\\frac{\\left(\\frac{10}{3}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{5}{3}\\right)}=2.\n$$" "Recall that $\\text{Col}(A)$ is the span of the columns of $A.$ \n\nFirst, let's denote\n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{(-3)\\cdot 2+4\\cdot (-1)+(-1)\\cdot 4}{{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{4}^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{2}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{5}{3} \\\\ \\frac{10}{3} \\\\ \\frac{5}{3}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $$\n\\displaystyle B=\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{5}{3} \\\\ \\frac{10}{3} \\\\ \\frac{5}{3}\\end{bmatrix}\\}\n$$ is an orthogonal basis for $\\text{Col}(A).$\n\nFinally, $b=\\frac{10}{3},$ $c=\\frac{5}{3},$ and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{b}{c}=\\frac{\\left(\\frac{10}{3}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{5}{3}\\right)}=2.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186326874 :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{Col}(A)$ is the span of the columns of $A.$  \n\nFirst, let's denote\n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{(-3)\\cdot 1+3\\cdot (-1)+(-3)\\cdot 2}{{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{2}^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we normalize each vector to get an orthonormal basis:\n\n- ${u}_{1}=\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{2}^{2}}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3}\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- ${u}_{2}=\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nTherefore, $B={{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}={\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{bmatrix}}$ is an orthonormal basis for $\\text{Col}(A).$\n\nFinally, $a=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3},$ $b=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3},$ $c=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}$ and  \n\n$$\n|a|+|b|+|c|=|-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}|+|\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}|+|\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}|=\\sqrt{3}.\n$$" "Recall that $\\text{Col}(A)$ is the span of the columns of $A.$  \n\nFirst, let's denote\n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{(-3)\\cdot 1+3\\cdot (-1)+(-3)\\cdot 2}{{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{2}^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we normalize each vector to get an orthonormal basis:\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {u}_{1}=\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{2}^{2}}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {u}_{2}=\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{