2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}\n$$ is an orthogonal basis for $V.$\n\nFinally, $a=3,$ $b=2,$ and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186326899 :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{Col}(A)$ is the span of the columns of $A.$  \n\nFirst, let's denote\n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ \\sqrt{3} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ \\sqrt{3} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{4\\cdot 3+0\\cdot \\sqrt{3}+7\\cdot 0}{{3}^{2}+(\\sqrt{3})^{2}+{0}^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ \\sqrt{3} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -\\sqrt{3} \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B=\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ \\sqrt{3} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -\\sqrt{3} \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}\\}$ is an orthogonal basis for $\\text{Col}(A).$\n\nFinally, $a=1,$ $b=-\\sqrt{3},$ and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{-\\sqrt{3}}{1}=-\\sqrt{3}.\n$$" "Recall that $\\text{Col}(A)$ is the span of the columns of $A.$  \n\nFirst, let's denote\n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ \\sqrt{3} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ \\sqrt{3} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{