in{aligned}-14 & 11 \\\\ -34 & -k\\end{aligned}|=-4|\\begin{aligned}9 & 11 \\\\ 6 & -k\\end{aligned}|\n$$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186329690 :question/problem "$|\\begin{aligned}1 & \\sqrt{2} & -1 & 10 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -3.5 & 48 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle |\\begin{aligned}1 & \\sqrt{2} & -1 & 10 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -3.5 & 48 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186329693 :question/problem "Calculate the value of \n$\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -3 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 2 & -2 & 4 \\\\ -2 & -2 & 3 & 2\\end{aligned}|.$\n\n*Hint: Use elementary row operations to make the entries below ${a}_{12}$ equal to zero. You should be able to reduce the given $4\\times 4$ determinant to a $3\\times 3$ determinant.*" "Calculate the value of \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -3 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 2 & -2 & 4 \\\\ -2 & -2 & 3 & 2\\end{aligned}|.\n$$\n\n*Hint: Use elementary row operations to make the entries below ${a}_{12}$ equal to zero. You should be able to reduce the given $4\\times 4$ determinant to a $3\\times 3$ determinant.*"] [:db/cas 17592186329694 :question/problem "$|\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\pi  & -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\sqrt{5} & 1.5 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2 & \\pi  & 1\\end{aligned}|=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle |\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\pi  & -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\sqrt{5} & 1.5 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2 & \\pi  & 1\\end{aligned}|=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186329698 :question/problem "Find the determinant of the matrix \n$K=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -2 & \\,-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 8 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.$" "Find the determinant of the matrix \n$$\n\\displaystyle K=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -2 & \\,-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 8 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186329702 :question/worked-solution "The determinant of the coefficient matrix is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-3-10 \\\\  & -13.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to Cramer's rule, the value of $x$ is given by $x=\\frac{{D}_{x}}{D},$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{x} & =|\\begin{aligned}k & 5 \\\\ -4 & -1\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{{D}_{x}}{D} \\\\ -1 & =\\frac{|\\begin{aligned}k & 5 \\\\ -4 & -1\\end{aligned}|}{-13} \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}k & 5 \\\\ -4 & -1\\end{aligned}| & =(-1)(-13) \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}k & 5 \\\\ -4 & -1\\end{aligned}| & =13\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the correct answer is $|\\begin{aligned}k & 5 \\\\ -4 & -1\\end{aligned}|=13$ only." "The determinant of the coefficient matrix is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-3-10 \\\\  & -13.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to Cramer's rule, the value of $x$ is given by $x=\\frac{{D}_{x}}{D},$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{x} & =|\\begin{aligned}k & 5 \\\\ -4 & -1\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{{D}_{x}}{D} \\\\ -1 & =\\frac{|\\begin{aligned}k & 5 \\\\ -4 & -1\\end{aligned}|}{-13} \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}k & 5 \\\\ -4 & -1\\end{aligned}| & =(-1)(-13) \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}k & 5 \\\\ -4 & -1\\end{aligned}| & =13\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle |\\begin{aligned}k & 5 \\\\ -4 & -1\\end{aligned}|=13\n$$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186329713 :question/problem "$\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta })=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta })=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186329714 :question/problem "Simplify $\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }+\\frac{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }.$" "Simplify $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }+\\frac{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186329716 :question/problem "$\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }({sin}^{3}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }({sin}^{3}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186329717 :question/problem "Simplify $\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta -1}-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta +1}.$" "Simplify $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta -1}-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta +1}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186329718 :question/problem "$\\operatorname{sin}⁡t(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t})=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{sin}⁡t(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t})=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186329722 :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{36-{x}^{2}}}\\text{d}x\\,=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{36-{x}^{2}}}\\text{d}x\\,=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186329765 :question/problem "Find ${f}^{′}(2)$ if $f(x)={\\int }_{2}^{2x}\\sqrt{{t}^{3}-t}\\,\\text{d}t.$" "Find ${f}^{′}(2)$ if $$\n\\displaystyle f(x)={\\int }_{2}^{2x}\\sqrt{{t}^{3}-t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186329770 :question/worked-solution "As $\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =\\frac{5}{3}$ then $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }=\\frac{3}{5}.$\n\nLet's sketch our angle $\\theta $ in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](images/aa/aaba5af0542d29ef91d4b0e17907a81e32bccc402c48b32c205e7298630b5bc0.png)\n\nWe're given that $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{3}{5}$ We can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{3}{5}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{9}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{16}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the negative answer since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ is negative in the second quadrant. So, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =-\\frac{4}{5}.$\n\nFinally, knowing that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta },$ and $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }$ we have:\n\n- $\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\left(-\\frac{4}{5}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right)}=-\\frac{4}{3}$\n\n- $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right)}{\\left(-\\frac{4}{5}\\right)}=-\\frac{3}{4}.$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta =-\\frac{4}{3}-\\frac{3}{4}=-\\frac{25}{12}.$" "As $\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =\\frac{5}{3}$ then $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }=\\frac{3}{5}.\n$$\n\nLet's sketch our angle $\\theta $ in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](images/aa/aaba5af0542d29ef91d4b0e17907a81e32bccc402c48b32c205e7298630b5bc0.png)\n\nWe're given that $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{3}{5}$ We can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{3}{5}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{9}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{16}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the negative answer since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ is negative in the second quadrant. So, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =-\\frac{4}{5}.$\n\nFinally, knowing that $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta },\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\n$$ we have:\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\left(-\\frac{4}{5}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right)}=-\\frac{4}{3}\n$$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right)}{\\left(-\\frac{4}{5}\\right)}=-\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta =-\\frac{4}{3}-\\frac{3}{4}=-\\frac{25}{12}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186329799 :question/worked-solution "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is ${x}^{3}.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by ${x}^{3},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}+2x-3}{{x}^{3}-x} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{2x}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{3}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{x}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{3}{{x}^{3}})}{(1-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $\\frac{1}{x},\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}},\\frac{3}{{x}^{3}}$ and $\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{3}{{x}^{3}})}{(1-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & \\to \\frac{0+0-0}{1-0} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the horizontal asymptote is $y=0.$" "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is ${x}^{3}.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by ${x}^{3},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}+2x-3}{{x}^{3}-x} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{2x}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{3}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{x}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{3}{{x}^{3}})}{(1-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{x},\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}},\\frac{3}{{x}^{3}}\n$$ and $\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{3}{{x}^{3}})}{(1-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & \\to \\frac{0+0-0}{1-0} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the horizontal asymptote is $y=0.$"] [:db/cas 17592186329800 :question/worked-solution "The intermediate value theorem can be applied on closed intervals on which the function is continuous. \n\nThe function $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}$ is discontinuous at the values of $x$ which make the denominator $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ equal to zero. Therefore, $f(x)$ is discontinuous at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi $, where $n$ is any integer.\n\n- On the interval $[0,1],$ we **can** apply the intermediate value theorem to $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.$\n\n- On the interval $[1,2],$ we **cannot** apply the intermediate value theorem to $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ because this interval contains the point of discontinuity $\\frac{\\pi }{2}≈1.57\\,.$\n\n- On the interval $[-1,0),$ we **cannot** apply the intermediate value theorem to $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ because this interval is not closed.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $[0,1]$ only." "The intermediate value theorem can be applied on closed intervals on which the function is continuous. \n\nThe function $$\n\\displaystyle f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\n$$ is discontinuous at the values of $x$ which make the denominator $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ equal to zero. Therefore, $f(x)$ is discontinuous at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi $, where $n$ is any integer.\n\n- On the interval $[0,1],$ we **can** apply the intermediate value theorem to $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.$\n\n- On the interval $[1,2],$ we **cannot** apply the intermediate value theorem to $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ because this interval contains the point of discontinuity $\\frac{\\pi }{2}≈1.57\\,.$\n\n- On the interval $[-1,0),$ we **cannot** apply the intermediate value theorem to $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ because this interval is not closed.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $[0,1]$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186329807 :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x)$ is a  function such that ${\\int }_{5}^{12}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=12$ and ${\\int }_{5}^{-5}6\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=18.$ What is the value of ${\\int }_{-5}^{12}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x?$" "Suppose that $f(x)$ is a  function such that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{5}^{12}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=12\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{5}^{-5}6\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=18.\n$$ What is the value of $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-5}^{12}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186329808 :question/problem "Let $f$ and $g$ be continuous functions such that ${\\int }_{0}^{7}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=18,{\\int }_{0}^{7}\\frac{1}{2}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x=5,$ and ${\\int }_{3}^{7}(f(x)-g(x))\\text{d}x=4.$ What is the value of ${\\int }_{0}^{3}(f(x)-g(x))\\text{d}x?$" "Let $f$ and $g$ be continuous functions such that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{7}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=18,{\\int }_{0}^{7}\\frac{1}{2}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x=5,\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{3}^{7}(f(x)-g(x))\\text{d}x=4.\n$$ What is the value of $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{3}(f(x)-g(x))\\text{d}x?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186329808 :question/worked-solution "Let's simplify one of the given integrals:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{7}\\frac{1}{2}\\,g(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =5 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{7}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =5 \\\\ {\\int }_{0}^{7}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the adjacent intervals rule and substituting the known values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{7}(f(x)-g(x))\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(f(x)-g(x))\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{3}^{7}(f(x)-g(x))\\text{d}x \\\\ {\\int }_{0}^{7}(f(x)-g(x))\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(f(x)-g(x))\\text{d}x+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by applying the sum rule for definite integrals and substituting the known values, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{7}(f(x)-g(x))\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(f(x)-g(x))\\text{d}x+4 \\\\ {\\int }_{0}^{7}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x-{\\int }_{0}^{7}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(f(x)-g(x))\\text{d}x+4 \\\\ 18-10 & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(f(x)-g(x))\\text{d}x+4 \\\\ 4 & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(f(x)-g(x))\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${\\int }_{0}^{3}(f(x)-g(x))\\text{d}x=4.$" "Let's simplify one of the given integrals:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{7}\\frac{1}{2}\\,g(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =5 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{7}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =5 \\\\ {\\int }_{0}^{7}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the adjacent intervals rule and substituting the known values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{7}(f(x)-g(x))\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(f(x)-g(x))\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{3}^{7}(f(x)-g(x))\\text{d}x \\\\ {\\int }_{0}^{7}(f(x)-g(x))\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(f(x)-g(x))\\text{d}x+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by applying the sum rule for definite integrals and substituting the known values, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{7}(f(x)-g(x))\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(f(x)-g(x))\\text{d}x+4 \\\\ {\\int }_{0}^{7}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x-{\\int }_{0}^{7}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(f(x)-g(x))\\text{d}x+4 \\\\ 18-10 & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(f(x)-g(x))\\text{d}x+4 \\\\ 4 & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(f(x)-g(x))\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{3}(f(x)-g(x))\\text{d}x=4.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186329820 :question/worked-solution "The invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\text{Col}(A) &={R}^{n} \\\\ \\text{dim(Col}(A)) &=n=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\ \\text{Null}(A) &=\\{0\\} \\\\ \\text{dim(Null}(A)) &=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\\right.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the options separately.\n\n- The following statement is true:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $det(A)=0,$ then $A$ has no inverse.* $\\,✓$  \n\nIndeed,  a matrix $A$ is invertible if and only if $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\n- The following statement is false:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $det(A)=0,$ then $\\text{rank}(A)=n.$*$\\,\\times$\n\nSince our matrix is not invertible, according to the invertible matrix theorem, it must be $\\text{rank}(A)\\ne n.$\n\n- The following statement is false:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $det(A)=0,$ then all columns of $A$ are pivot columns.*  $\\,\\times$\n\nSince $\\text{rank}(A)\\ne n$, it must be $\\text{dim(Col}(A))<n,$ which means that not all columns of $A$ are pivot columns.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$A$ has no inverse” only." "The invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $$\n\\displaystyle \\,⇔\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\text{Col}(A) &={R}^{n} \\\\ \\text{dim(Col}(A)) &=n=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\ \\text{Null}(A) &=\\{0\\} \\\\ \\text{dim(Null}(A)) &=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the options separately.\n\n- The following statement is true:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $det(A)=0,$ then $A$ has no inverse.* $\\,✓$  \n\nIndeed,  a matrix $A$ is invertible if and only if $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\n- The following statement is false:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $det(A)=0,$ then $\\text{rank}(A)=n.$*$\\,\\times$\n\nSince our matrix is not invertible, according to the invertible matrix theorem, it must be $\\text{rank}(A)\\ne n.$\n\n- The following statement is false:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $det(A)=0,$ then all columns of $A$ are pivot columns.*  $\\,\\times$\n\nSince $\\text{rank}(A)\\ne n$, it must be $\\text{dim(Col}(A))<n,$ which means that not all columns of $A$ are pivot columns.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$A$ has no inverse” only."] [:db/cas 17592186329832 :question/problem "For $x>0,$ find ${f}^{′}(x)$ if $f(x)={\\int }_{-{x}^{2}}^{-4}\\frac{1}{t}\\,\\text{d}t.$" "For $x>0,$ find ${f}^{′}(x)$ if $$\n\\displaystyle f(x)={\\int }_{-{x}^{2}}^{-4}\\frac{1}{t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186329836 :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x)$ is a  function such that ${\\int }_{2}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=9$ and ${\\int }_{9}^{6}4\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=20.$ What is the value of ${\\int }_{2}^{9}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x?$" "Suppose that $f(x)$ is a  function such that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{2}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=9\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{9}^{6}4\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=20.\n$$ What is the value of $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{2}^{9}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186329837 :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x)$ is a function such that ${\\int }_{3}^{0}\\frac{1}{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-1$ and ${\\int }_{3}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=6.$ What is the value of ${\\int }_{0}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x?$" "Suppose that $f(x)$ is a function such that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{3}^{0}\\frac{1}{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-1\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{3}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=6.\n$$ What is the value of $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186329842 :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x)$ is a  function such that ${\\int }_{2}^{7}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=8$ and ${\\int }_{2}^{0}2f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=4.$ What is the value of ${\\int }_{0}^{7}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x?$" "Suppose that $f(x)$ is a  function such that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{2}^{7}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=8\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{2}^{0}2f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=4.\n$$ What is the value of $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{7}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186329843 :question/problem "Suppose that $g(x)$ is a  function such that  ${\\int }_{4}^{8}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x=5$ and ${\\int }_{4}^{1}3g(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-6.$  What is the value of ${\\int }_{1}^{8}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x?$" "Suppose that $g(x)$ is a  function such that  $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{4}^{8}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x=5\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{4}^{1}3g(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-6.\n$$  What is the value of $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{8}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186329844 :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x)$ is a  function such that ${\\int }_{2}^{-2}\\frac{1}{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-3$ and ${\\int }_{2}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=10.$  What is the value of ${\\int }_{-2}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x?$" "Suppose that $f(x)$ is a  function such that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{2}^{-2}\\frac{1}{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-3\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{2}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=10.\n$$  What is the value of $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-2}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186329893 :question/worked-solution "Let $r$  be the radius, $A$ the area, and $C$ the circumference of the circle. We wish to find $r$ at the moment when $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{2}{3}\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nTo start, let's set up an equation relating the area $A$ and the circumference $C$ of the circle. Since $C=2\\pi r$ and $A=\\pi {r}^{2},$ the relationship between $A$ and $C$ is\n\n$$\nA=\\pi {r}^{2}=\\pi {\\left(\\frac{C}{2\\pi }\\right)}^{2}=\\frac{{C}^{2}}{4\\pi }.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{{C}^{2}}{4\\pi }\\right) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}C}\\left(\\frac{{C}^{2}}{4\\pi }\\right) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{C}{2\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{2}{3}\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{C}{2\\pi } \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & =\\frac{C}{2\\pi } \\\\ C & =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, since $C=2\\pi r,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\pi r & =\\frac{4\\pi }{3} \\\\ r & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let $r$  be the radius, $A$ the area, and $C$ the circumference of the circle. We wish to find $r$ at the moment when $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{2}{3}\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\nTo start, let's set up an equation relating the area $A$ and the circumference $C$ of the circle. Since $C=2\\pi r$ and $A=\\pi {r}^{2},$ the relationship between $A$ and $C$ is\n\n$$\nA=\\pi {r}^{2}=\\pi {\\left(\\frac{C}{2\\pi }\\right)}^{2}=\\frac{{C}^{2}}{4\\pi }.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{{C}^{2}}{4\\pi }\\right) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}C}\\left(\\frac{{C}^{2}}{4\\pi }\\right) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{C}{2\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{2}{3}\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t},\n$$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{C}{2\\pi } \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & =\\frac{C}{2\\pi } \\\\ C & =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, since $C=2\\pi r,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\pi r & =\\frac{4\\pi }{3} \\\\ r & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186329894 :question/worked-solution "Let $r$  be the radius, $A$ the area, and $D$ the diameter of the circle. We wish to find $r$ at the moment when $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{1}{3}\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nTo start, let's set up an equation relating the area $A$ and the diameter $D$ of the circle. Since $D=2r$ and $A=\\pi {r}^{2},$ the relationship between $A$ and $D$ is\n\n$$\nA=\\pi {r}^{2}=\\pi {\\left(\\frac{D}{2}\\right)}^{2}=\\frac{\\pi {D}^{2}}{4}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{\\pi {D}^{2}}{4}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}D}\\left(\\frac{\\pi {D}^{2}}{4}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi D}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{1}{3}\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi D}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & =\\frac{\\pi D}{2} \\\\ D & =\\frac{2}{3\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, since $D=2r,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2r & =\\frac{2}{3\\pi } \\\\ r & =\\frac{1}{3\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let $r$  be the radius, $A$ the area, and $D$ the diameter of the circle. We wish to find $r$ at the moment when $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{1}{3}\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\nTo start, let's set up an equation relating the area $A$ and the diameter $D$ of the circle. Since $D=2r$ and $A=\\pi {r}^{2},$ the relationship between $A$ and $D$ is\n\n$$\nA=\\pi {r}^{2}=\\pi {\\left(\\frac{D}{2}\\right)}^{2}=\\frac{\\pi {D}^{2}}{4}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{\\pi {D}^{2}}{4}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}D}\\left(\\frac{\\pi {D}^{2}}{4}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi D}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{1}{3}\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t},\n$$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi D}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & =\\frac{\\pi D}{2} \\\\ D & =\\frac{2}{3\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, since $D=2r,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2r & =\\frac{2}{3\\pi } \\\\ r & =\\frac{1}{3\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186329930 :question/problem "For the functions $f(x)$ and $g(x),$ you are given the following data:\n\n$$\nf(1)=8,\\,{f}^{′}(1)=6,\\,g(1)=4,\\,{g}^{′}(1)=4\n$$\n\nCompute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}\\right)$ at $x=1.$" "For the functions $f(x)$ and $g(x),$ you are given the following data:\n\n$$\nf(1)=8,\\,{f}^{′}(1)=6,\\,g(1)=4,\\,{g}^{′}(1)=4\n$$\n\nCompute $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}\\right)\n$$ at $x=1.$"] [:db/cas 17592186329944 :question/problem "The values of functions $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ and their derivatives at $x=3$ are given in the table below. \n\n| $x$ | $f(x)$ | $g(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ | ${g}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $3$ | $2$ | $-1$ | $-4$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n\nCompute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}\\right)$ at $x=3.$" "The values of functions $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ and their derivatives at $x=3$ are given in the table below. \n\n| $x$ | $f(x)$ | $g(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ | ${g}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $3$ | $2$ | $-1$ | $-4$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n\nCompute $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}\\right)\n$$ at $x=3.$"] [:db/cas 17592186332661 :question/problem "Calculate $\\int csc⁡x(3cot⁡x+2csc⁡x)\\text{d}x.$" "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int csc⁡x(3cot⁡x+2csc⁡x)\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332662 :question/problem "Calculate $\\int sec⁡x(7sec⁡x+4tan⁡x)\\text{d}x.$" "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int sec⁡x(7sec⁡x+4tan⁡x)\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332663 :question/problem "$\\int (\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}-6{x}^{2})\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int (\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}-6{x}^{2})\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332664 :question/problem "$\\int (\\frac{4}{1+{x}^{2}}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int (\\frac{4}{1+{x}^{2}}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332665 :question/problem "$\\int (\\frac{6}{1+{x}^{2}}-9)\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int (\\frac{6}{1+{x}^{2}}-9)\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332667 :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}-7}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{{x}^{2}-7}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332668 :question/problem "![](images/96/96622ea1476cf79ec3ff6d6486d77eda465de80d005304f542b4aadfb6be2b7a.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x),$ shown above, consists of two line segments. Find the value of ${\\int }_{-3}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" "![](images/96/96622ea1476cf79ec3ff6d6486d77eda465de80d005304f542b4aadfb6be2b7a.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x),$ shown above, consists of two line segments. Find the value of $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-3}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332669 :question/problem "![](images/55/555b702c1903f8cc4e379ad4281aa86708757755e4f9fb14b0ec7790a80a17dd.png)\n\nGiven the graph of $y=f(x)$ above, find the value of \n${\\int }_{-4}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" "![](images/55/555b702c1903f8cc4e379ad4281aa86708757755e4f9fb14b0ec7790a80a17dd.png)\n\nGiven the graph of $y=f(x)$ above, find the value of \n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-4}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332671 :question/problem "What is ${\\int }_{2}^{6}{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x$ expressed as the limit of a left Riemann sum?" "What is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{2}^{6}{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ expressed as the limit of a left Riemann sum?"] [:db/cas 17592186332672 :question/problem "What is ${\\int }_{3}^{4}5{x}^{2}\\text{d}x$ expressed as the limit of a right Riemann sum?" "What is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{3}^{4}5{x}^{2}\\text{d}x\n$$ expressed as the limit of a right Riemann sum?"] [:db/cas 17592186332673 :question/problem "![](images/90/90ab7f470adc629be8c089cb74a259b9663c463cf224251b099cae33044b1ac5.png)\n\nConsider the graph of the function $y=f(x),$ shown above. It consists of a line segment and an arc of a quarter-circle centered at $(0,0)$ with radius $2.$ Find the value of ${\\int }_{-3}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" "![](images/90/90ab7f470adc629be8c089cb74a259b9663c463cf224251b099cae33044b1ac5.png)\n\nConsider the graph of the function $y=f(x),$ shown above. It consists of a line segment and an arc of a quarter-circle centered at $(0,0)$ with radius $2.$ Find the value of $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-3}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332674 :question/problem "![](images/5d/5d6f61767434332bbb92574ff87d72bda4b07028d77bda5925f73b32a7c751d5.png)\n\nThe graph of a function $y=f(x),$ shown above. It consists of a line segment and an arc of a semi-circle centered at $(2,-2)$ with radius $2.$ Find the value of ${\\int }_{-4}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" "![](images/5d/5d6f61767434332bbb92574ff87d72bda4b07028d77bda5925f73b32a7c751d5.png)\n\nThe graph of a function $y=f(x),$ shown above. It consists of a line segment and an arc of a semi-circle centered at $(2,-2)$ with radius $2.$ Find the value of $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-4}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332675 :question/problem "![](images/22/22472e04f7bd26076da43a2ad17a192726ad19b5bd3885ce066c9523cad4f870.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x),$ shown above, consists of three line segments. Find the value of ${\\int }_{-2}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" "![](images/22/22472e04f7bd26076da43a2ad17a192726ad19b5bd3885ce066c9523cad4f870.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x),$ shown above, consists of three line segments. Find the value of $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-2}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332676 :question/problem "![](images/78/78500fa72c8f6aa49d95f34b88ce71ec79304203d7646200e98c311d8610200f.png)\n\nGiven the graph of $y=f(x)$ above, find the value of \n${\\int }_{-4}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" "![](images/78/78500fa72c8f6aa49d95f34b88ce71ec79304203d7646200e98c311d8610200f.png)\n\nGiven the graph of $y=f(x)$ above, find the value of \n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-4}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332677 :question/problem "![](images/11/11cf2a28b6ce6281de0299a4a585bc3c98bf891f6d958401f22aab2fc6ce5ef2.png)\n\nGiven the graph of $y=f(x)$ above, find the value of ${\\int }_{-4}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" "![](images/11/11cf2a28b6ce6281de0299a4a585bc3c98bf891f6d958401f22aab2fc6ce5ef2.png)\n\nGiven the graph of $y=f(x)$ above, find the value of $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-4}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332678 :question/problem "![](images/e0/e0fc07ac8466381b668877e25cd0c9d8d5258a9061548e7b8efb12f75f6a0f04.png)\n\nThe graph of a function $y=f(x)$ is shown above. It consists of two lines and the arc of a quarter-circle of radius $2.$ Given $g(t)={\\int }_{-2}^{t}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x,$ determine the value of $g(3).$" "![](images/e0/e0fc07ac8466381b668877e25cd0c9d8d5258a9061548e7b8efb12f75f6a0f04.png)\n\nThe graph of a function $y=f(x)$ is shown above. It consists of two lines and the arc of a quarter-circle of radius $2.$ Given $$\n\\displaystyle g(t)={\\int }_{-2}^{t}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$ determine the value of $g(3).$"] [:db/cas 17592186332679 :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{-4}^{-1}\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-4}^{-1}\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332680 :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{-2}^{-1}\\frac{1}{{x}^{5}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-2}^{-1}\\frac{1}{{x}^{5}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332681 :question/problem "Find the function $f(x)$ and the value of $b,$ given that ${\\int }_{e}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e+\\frac{ie}{n})\\cdot \\frac{e}{n}].$" "Find the function $f(x)$ and the value of $b,$ given that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{e}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e+\\frac{ie}{n})\\cdot \\frac{e}{n}].\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332683 :question/problem "Given that ${\\int }_{0}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-5$ and ${\\int }_{4}^{7}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=8$ then what is ${\\int }_{0}^{7}(7+3f(x))\\,\\text{d}x?$" "Given that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-5\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{4}^{7}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=8\n$$ then what is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{7}(7+3f(x))\\,\\text{d}x?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332684 :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{sec}⁡3x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{\\operatorname{sec}⁡3x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332685 :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{\\pi /8}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{{sin}^{2}⁡(2x)}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{\\pi /8}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{{sin}^{2}⁡(2x)}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332686 :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{4}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{4}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332686 :question/worked-solution "We use the identity $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{4}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We use the identity $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\n$$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{4}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332687 :question/problem "$\\int \\operatorname{cot}⁡x(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\operatorname{cot}⁡x(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332687 :question/worked-solution "We multiply out the parentheses and then use the identities $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x},$ and $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\operatorname{cot}⁡x(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-x+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We multiply out the parentheses and then use the identities $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x},\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x},\n$$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\operatorname{cot}⁡x(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-x+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332688 :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332688 :question/worked-solution "We use the identity $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We use the identity $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x},\n$$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332689 :question/problem "$\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x{sec}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x{sec}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332690 :question/problem "$\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x\\operatorname{sec}⁡3x\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x\\operatorname{sec}⁡3x\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332691 :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡4x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡4x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332691 :question/worked-solution "We can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡4x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\operatorname{tan}⁡4x\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe then use the fact that $\\operatorname{tan}⁡4x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x}$ to express this as\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the identity $\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x}=\\operatorname{sec}⁡4x$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡4x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {sec}^{2}⁡4x\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this by substitution. Let $u=4x.$ Then $\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{4}\\text{d}u.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡4x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {sec}^{2}⁡4x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {sec}^{2}⁡u\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int {sec}^{2}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{tan}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{tan}⁡4x+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡4x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\operatorname{tan}⁡4x\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe then use the fact that $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{tan}⁡4x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x}\n$$ to express this as\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the identity $\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x}=\\operatorname{sec}⁡4x$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡4x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {sec}^{2}⁡4x\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this by substitution. Let $u=4x.$ Then $\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{4}\\text{d}u.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡4x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {sec}^{2}⁡4x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {sec}^{2}⁡u\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int {sec}^{2}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{tan}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{tan}⁡4x+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332692 :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{1}{{sec}^{2}⁡2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{1}{{sec}^{2}⁡2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332693 :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332693 :question/worked-solution "We can write the integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\text{d}x={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nWe use the fact that $\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ and $\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ to express this as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡x{|}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)-\\operatorname{sec}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}-1.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We can write the integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\text{d}x={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nWe use the fact that $\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ and $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\n$$ to express this as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡x{|}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)-\\operatorname{sec}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}-1.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332697 :question/problem "![](images/ed/edcecf885aaf18a3aa6f6726c0485b1885e37132e73c8964de2576f7da5d61bf.png)\n\nThe graph of a function $y=f(x)$ is shown above. It consists of two lines and the arc of a quarter-circle of radius $2.$ Given $g(t)={\\int }_{-3}^{t}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x,$ determine the value of $g(0).$" "![](images/ed/edcecf885aaf18a3aa6f6726c0485b1885e37132e73c8964de2576f7da5d61bf.png)\n\nThe graph of a function $y=f(x)$ is shown above. It consists of two lines and the arc of a quarter-circle of radius $2.$ Given $$\n\\displaystyle g(t)={\\int }_{-3}^{t}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$ determine the value of $g(0).$"] [:db/cas 17592186332698 :question/problem "![](images/28/288b3eeaf1168316f2d8f43fa9484032daa8c9006a1c8355b8edfd1186dc2add.png)\n\nThe graph of a function $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Given $g(t)={\\int }_{-4}^{t}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x,$ determine the value of $g(2).$" "![](images/28/288b3eeaf1168316f2d8f43fa9484032daa8c9006a1c8355b8edfd1186dc2add.png)\n\nThe graph of a function $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Given $$\n\\displaystyle g(t)={\\int }_{-4}^{t}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x,\n