_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /2}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Evaluate  $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /2}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332762 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral  ${\\int }_{0}^{1/2}x{e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Evaluate the integral  $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1/2}x{e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332763 :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332764 :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{75}{|x+2|\\sqrt{(x+2)^{2}-25}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{75}{|x+2|\\sqrt{(x+2)^{2}-25}}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332765 :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{1}^{e}{log}_{4}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{e}{log}_{4}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332766 :question/problem "${\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{4}}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{4}}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332771 :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{2x+6}{{x}^{2}-4}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{2x+6}{{x}^{2}-4}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332772 :question/problem "${\\int }_{-6}^{-5}\\frac{5x+29}{(x+7)(x+4)}\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-6}^{-5}\\frac{5x+29}{(x+7)(x+4)}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332773 :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{-7}^{-3}\\frac{11x+46}{(x+2)(x+8)}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-7}^{-3}\\frac{11x+46}{(x+2)(x+8)}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332774 :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{-2}^{-1}\\frac{x+1}{x(x+3)}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-2}^{-1}\\frac{x+1}{x(x+3)}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332775 :question/problem "Given that\n\n$$\n\\frac{5{x}^{2}+6}{{x}^{2}(2x+3)}=-\\frac{4}{3x}+\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{23}{3(2x+3)},\n$$\n\ncalculate $\\int \\frac{5{x}^{2}+6}{(2x+3){x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Given that\n\n$$\n\\frac{5{x}^{2}+6}{{x}^{2}(2x+3)}=-\\frac{4}{3x}+\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{23}{3(2x+3)},\n$$\n\ncalculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{5{x}^{2}+6}{(2x+3){x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332777 :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams gives the slope field at the points indicated for the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2{x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}?$" "Which of the following diagrams gives the slope field at the points indicated for the differential equation $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2{x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332778 :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{3{x}^{2}+2x+1}{x({x}^{2}+x+1)}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{3{x}^{2}+2x+1}{x({x}^{2}+x+1)}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332780 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to \n${\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{{x}^{3}+1}?$" "Which of the following is equivalent to \n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{{x}^{3}+1}?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332781 :question/problem "${\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{4}{\\sqrt[5]{x}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{4}{\\sqrt[5]{x}}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332782 :question/problem "${\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{6}{\\sqrt[7]{x}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{6}{\\sqrt[7]{x}}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332783 :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{5}^{6}\\frac{{x}^{2}-9x+21}{(x-4)^{2}(3-x)}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{5}^{6}\\frac{{x}^{2}-9x+21}{(x-4)^{2}(3-x)}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332784 :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{3}^{4}\\frac{4x}{(x-2)^{2}}\\text{d}x.$" "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{3}^{4}\\frac{4x}{(x-2)^{2}}\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332785 :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{6x+12}{x(x+3)}\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{6x+12}{x(x+3)}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332787 :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{2}{3{x}^{2}+x}\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{2}{3{x}^{2}+x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332788 :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the given integrals.\n\n- Integral ${\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{{x}^{2}-9}\\,$ is an improper integral of the second kind since it has finite limits, and \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-9}\\to -\\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to {3}^{-}.\n$$\n\n- Integral ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-4}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{{x}^{2}-9}\\,$ is an improper integral over an *infinite* domain since its lower limit is infinite.\n\n- Integral ${\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{{x}^{2}}$ is not an improper integral since it has finite limits and the function under the integral is finite at every point $x\\in [1,3].$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{{x}^{2}-9}\\,$ only." "Let's examine each of the given integrals.\n\n- Integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{{x}^{2}-9}\\,\n$$ is an improper integral of the second kind since it has finite limits, and \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-9}\\to -\\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to {3}^{-}.\n$$\n\n- Integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-4}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{{x}^{2}-9}\\,\n$$ is an improper integral over an *infinite* domain since its lower limit is infinite.\n\n- Integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{{x}^{2}}\n$$ is not an improper integral since it has finite limits and the function under the integral is finite at every point $x\\in [1,3].$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{{x}^{2}-9}\\,\n$$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186332789 :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{-3{x}^{2}-18x+6}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{-3{x}^{2}-18x+6}}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332792 :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{e}^{3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{e}^{3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332792 :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the indefinite integral by $I=\\int {e}^{3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.$\n\nTo use integration by parts, let's suppose that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{3x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3{e}^{3x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int {e}^{3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\int (\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)(3{e}^{3x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-3\\int {e}^{3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-3J.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we can use integration by parts to evaluate\n\n$$\nJ=\\int {e}^{3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo do this, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{3x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3{e}^{3x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J & =\\int {e}^{3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{3x}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-\\int (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)(3{e}^{3x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+3\\int {e}^{3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+3I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, substituting this into our equation for $I,$ we can subsequently solve for $I:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={e}^{3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-3J \\\\ I & ={e}^{3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-3(-{e}^{3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+3I) \\\\ I & ={e}^{3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+3{e}^{3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-9I \\\\ 10I & ={e}^{3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+3{e}^{3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ I & =\\frac{{e}^{3x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate our definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{e}^{3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\left(\\frac{{e}^{3x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}{10}\\right){|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{3(2\\pi )}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi )+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi ))}{10}-\\frac{{e}^{3(0)}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0))}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{e}^{6\\pi }}{10}-\\frac{3}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{10}({e}^{6\\pi }-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's denote the indefinite integral by $$\n\\displaystyle I=\\int {e}^{3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo use integration by parts, let's suppose that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{3x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3{e}^{3x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int {e}^{3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\int (\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)(3{e}^{3x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-3\\int {e}^{3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-3J.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we can use integration by parts to evaluate\n\n$$\nJ=\\int {e}^{3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo do this, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{3x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3{e}^{3x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J & =\\int {e}^{3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{3x}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-\\int (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)(3{e}^{3x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+3\\int {e}^{3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+3I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, substituting this into our equation for $I,$ we can subsequently solve for $I:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={e}^{3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-3J \\\\ I & ={e}^{3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-3(-{e}^{3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+3I) \\\\ I & ={e}^{3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+3{e}^{3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-9I \\\\ 10I & ={e}^{3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+3{e}^{3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ I & =\\frac{{e}^{3x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate our definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{e}^{3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\left(\\frac{{e}^{3x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}{10}\\right){|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{3(2\\pi )}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi )+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi ))}{10}-\\frac{{e}^{3(0)}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0))}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{e}^{6\\pi }}{10}-\\frac{3}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{10}({e}^{6\\pi }-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332793 :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332793 :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the indefinite integral by $I=\\int {e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.$\n\nTo use integration by parts, let's suppose that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{-2x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-2{e}^{-2x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int {e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{-2x}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-\\int (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)(-2{e}^{-2x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2\\int {e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2J.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we can use integration by parts to evaluate\n\n$$\nJ=\\int {e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo do this, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{-2x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-2{e}^{-2x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J & =\\int {e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{-2x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)-\\int (\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)(-2{e}^{-2x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\int {e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, substituting this into our equation for $I,$ we can subsequently solve for $I:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =-{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2J \\\\ I & =-{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2({e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2I) \\\\ I & =-{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-4I \\\\ 5I & =-{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ I & =-\\frac{{e}^{-2x}}{5}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate our definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =(-\\frac{{e}^{-2x}}{5}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)){|}_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{-2(\\pi /2)}}{5}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right))+\\frac{{e}^{-2(-\\pi /2)}}{5}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2{e}^{-\\pi }}{5}-\\frac{2{e}^{\\pi }}{5} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{5}({e}^{\\pi }+{e}^{-\\pi })\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's denote the indefinite integral by $$\n\\displaystyle I=\\int {e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo use integration by parts, let's suppose that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{-2x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-2{e}^{-2x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int {e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{-2x}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-\\int (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)(-2{e}^{-2x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2\\int {e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2J.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we can use integration by parts to evaluate\n\n$$\nJ=\\int {e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo do this, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{-2x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-2{e}^{-2x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J & =\\int {e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{-2x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)-\\int (\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)(-2{e}^{-2x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\int {e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, substituting this into our equation for $I,$ we can subsequently solve for $I:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =-{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2J \\\\ I & =-{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2({e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2I) \\\\ I & =-{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-4I \\\\ 5I & =-{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ I & =-\\frac{{e}^{-2x}}{5}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate our definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =(-\\frac{{e}^{-2x}}{5}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)){|}_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{-2(\\pi /2)}}{5}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right))+\\frac{{e}^{-2(-\\pi /2)}}{5}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2{e}^{-\\pi }}{5}-\\frac{2{e}^{\\pi }}{5} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{5}({e}^{\\pi }+{e}^{-\\pi })\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332794 :question/problem "A curve has parametric equations $x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).$ Given that $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cot}⁡(2t),$ calculate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}$ at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{12}.$" "A curve has parametric equations $x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).$ Given that $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cot}⁡(2t),\n$$ calculate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}$ at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{12}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186332794 :question/worked-solution "To compute $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}},$ we can use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}\\left(\\frac{dy}{dx}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{dx}{dt}\\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cot}⁡(2t),$ and we can calculate $\\frac{dx}{dt}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{dx}{dt}=\\frac{d}{dt}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cot}⁡(2t))}{-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{csc}^{2}⁡(2t)}{-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}{csc}^{3}⁡(2t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{12},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}{|}_{t=\\pi /12} & =-\\frac{1}{4}{csc}^{3}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{12}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4{sin}^{3}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{3}} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To compute $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}},$ we can use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}\\left(\\frac{dy}{dx}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{dx}{dt}\\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cot}⁡(2t),\n$$ and we can calculate $\\frac{dx}{dt}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{dx}{dt}=\\frac{d}{dt}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cot}⁡(2t))}{-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{csc}^{2}⁡(2t)}{-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}{csc}^{3}⁡(2t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{12},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}{|}_{t=\\pi /12} & =-\\frac{1}{4}{csc}^{3}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{12}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4{sin}^{3}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{3}} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332810 :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{1}^{4}⟨4t-1,\\sqrt{t}⟩\\,\\text{d}t.$" "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{4}⟨4t-1,\\sqrt{t}⟩\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332812 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(5{x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Evaluate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(5{x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332812 :question/worked-solution "We need to use integration by parts. We'll work out the indefinite integral first, and then proceed to work out the definite integral.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=5{x}^{2}+1\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=10x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (5{x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(5{x}^{2}+1)(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-\\int (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)(10x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-(5{x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+10\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we use integration by parts to evaluate $\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x & \\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & \\,⟹\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,dx \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is an integral constant. \n\nSo, we get that the indefinite integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (5{x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-(5{x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+10\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-(5{x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+10(x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+D) \\\\  & =-(5{x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+10x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+10\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=10D.$ Finally then, we can work out the definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }5{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & ={[-(5{x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+10x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+10\\operatorname{cos}⁡x]}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =-(5{\\pi }^{2}+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi +10\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi +10\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi -(-(5(0)^{2}+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡0))-10\\cdot 0\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡0-10\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡0 \\\\  & =5{\\pi }^{2}+1-10+1-10 \\\\  & =5{\\pi }^{2}-18\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We need to use integration by parts. We'll work out the indefinite integral first, and then proceed to work out the definite integral.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=5{x}^{2}+1\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=10x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (5{x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(5{x}^{2}+1)(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-\\int (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)(10x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-(5{x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+10\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we use integration by parts to evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x & \\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & \\,⟹\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,dx \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is an integral constant. \n\nSo, we get that the indefinite integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (5{x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-(5{x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+10\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-(5{x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+10(x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+D) \\\\  & =-(5{x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+10x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+10\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=10D.$ Finally then, we can work out the definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }5{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & ={[-(5{x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+10x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+10\\operatorname{cos}⁡x]}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =-(5{\\pi }^{2}+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi +10\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi +10\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi -(-(5(0)^{2}+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡0))-10\\cdot 0\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡0-10\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡0 \\\\  & =5{\\pi }^{2}+1-10+1-10 \\\\  & =5{\\pi }^{2}-18\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332813 :question/problem "$\\int (4{x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int (4{x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332814 :question/problem "$\\int {x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-1)\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int {x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-1)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332814 :question/worked-solution "We need to use integration by parts. \n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={x}^{2}\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-1)\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-1)\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-1)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({x}^{2})(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-1))-\\int (\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-1)(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-1)-2\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-1)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we use integration by parts to evaluate $\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-1)\\text{d}x.$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-1)\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-1)\\,\\text{d}x=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-1)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(x)(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-1))-\\int (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-1))(1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-1)+\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-1)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-1)+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is an integral constant.\n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-1)\\,\\text{d}x & ={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-1)-2(-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-1)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-1)+D) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-1)+2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-1)-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-1)-2D \\\\  & =({x}^{2}-2)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-1)+2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-1)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=-2D.$" "We need to use integration by parts. \n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={x}^{2}\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-1)\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-1)\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-1)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({x}^{2})(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-1))-\\int (\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-1)(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-1)-2\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-1)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we use integration by parts to evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-1)\\text{d}x.\n$$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-1)\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-1)\\,\\text{d}x=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-1)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(x)(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-1))-\\int (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-1))(1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-1)+\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-1)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-1)+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is an integral constant.\n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-1)\\,\\text{d}x & ={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-1)-2(-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-1)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-1)+D) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-1)+2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-1)-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-1)-2D \\\\  & =({x}^{2}-2)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-1)+2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-1)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=-2D.$"] [:db/cas 17592186332823 :question/problem "The function $f(x)$ is defined below. What is the value of \n${\\int }_{-5}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x?$\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}x+2, & x<1 \\\\ 3, & x\\ge 1\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The function $f(x)$ is defined below. What is the value of \n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-5}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x?\n$$\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}x+2, & x<1 \\\\ 3, & x\\ge 1\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332824 :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each series in turn.\n\n- The series ${\\sum }_{n=5}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2}{\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{n}$ starts with $n=5.$ So the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{6}}{{a}_{5}} & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}{\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{6}}{\\frac{1}{2}{\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{5}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is convergent because $|r|<1.$\n\n- The series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }8{(2)}^{n}$ starts with $n=1.$ So the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{8{(2)}^{2}}{8{(2)}^{1}} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is divergent because $|r|\\ge 1.$\n\n- The series ${\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{8}^{n+1}}{{16}^{n-1}}$ starts with $n=0.$ So the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{{a}_{0}} & =\\frac{\\frac{{8}^{1+1}}{{16}^{1-1}}}{\\frac{{8}^{0+1}}{{16}^{0-1}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{{8}^{2}}{{16}^{0}}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{{8}^{1}}{{16}^{-1}}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{{8}^{2}}{{16}^{0}}\\right)\\left(\\frac{{16}^{-1}}{{8}^{1}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{{8}^{2}}{1}\\right)\\left(\\frac{1}{16\\cdot 8}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is convergent because $|r|<1.$\n\nIn conclusion, only series  ${\\sum }_{n=5}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2}{\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{n}$ and ${\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{8}^{n+1}}{{16}^{n-1}}$ converge." "Let's look at each series in turn.\n\n- The series $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=5}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2}{\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{n}\n$$ starts with $n=5.$ So the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{6}}{{a}_{5}} & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}{\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{6}}{\\frac{1}{2}{\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{5}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is convergent because $|r|<1.$\n\n- The series $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }8{(2)}^{n}\n$$ starts with $n=1.$ So the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{8{(2)}^{2}}{8{(2)}^{1}} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is divergent because $|r|\\ge 1.$\n\n- The series $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{8}^{n+1}}{{16}^{n-1}}\n$$ starts with $n=0.$ So the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{{a}_{0}} & =\\frac{\\frac{{8}^{1+1}}{{16}^{1-1}}}{\\frac{{8}^{0+1}}{{16}^{0-1}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{{8}^{2}}{{16}^{0}}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{{8}^{1}}{{16}^{-1}}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{{8}^{2}}{{16}^{0}}\\right)\\left(\\frac{{16}^{-1}}{{8}^{1}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{{8}^{2}}{1}\\right)\\left(\\frac{1}{16\\cdot 8}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is convergent because $|r|<1.$\n\nIn conclusion, only series  $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=5}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2}{\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{n}\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{8}^{n+1}}{{16}^{n-1}}\n$$ converge."] [:db/cas 17592186332827 :question/problem "For which values of $k$ does the series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{3}^{kn-2}}{{9}^{n-1}}$ converge?" "For which values of $k$ does the series $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{3}^{kn-2}}{{9}^{n-1}}\n$$ converge?"] [:db/cas 17592186332827 :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the series so that it's in the form ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }a{r}^{n},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{3}^{kn-2}}{{9}^{n-1}} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{3}^{kn}\\cdot {3}^{-2}}{{9}^{n-1}} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{9}\\left(\\frac{{3}^{kn}}{{9}^{n-1}}\\right) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\left(\\frac{{3}^{kn}}{{9}^{n}}\\right) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{\\left(\\frac{{3}^{k}}{9}\\right)}^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that this is a geometric series with a common ratio of $r=\\frac{{3}^{k}}{9}.$ \n\nFor the series to converge, we require $|r|<1.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\frac{{3}^{k}}{9}| & <1 \\\\ \\frac{{3}^{k}}{9} & <1 \\\\ {3}^{k} & <9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we could remove the absolute value bars because $\\frac{{3}^{k}}{9}$ is always positive.\n\nThe equation ${3}^{k}=9$ has the solution $k=2.$ Now, since ${3}^{k}$ increases as $k$ increases, the solution to ${3}^{k}<9$ must be $k<2.$" "First, we rewrite the series so that it's in the form $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }a{r}^{n},\n$$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{3}^{kn-2}}{{9}^{n-1}} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{3}^{kn}\\cdot {3}^{-2}}{{9}^{n-1}} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{9}\\left(\\frac{{3}^{kn}}{{9}^{n-1}}\\right) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\left(\\frac{{3}^{kn}}{{9}^{n}}\\right) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{\\left(\\frac{{3}^{k}}{9}\\right)}^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that this is a geometric series with a common ratio of $r=\\frac{{3}^{k}}{9}.$ \n\nFor the series to converge, we require $|r|<1.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\frac{{3}^{k}}{9}| & <1 \\\\ \\frac{{3}^{k}}{9} & <1 \\\\ {3}^{k} & <9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we could remove the absolute value bars because $\\frac{{3}^{k}}{9}$ is always positive.\n\nThe equation ${3}^{k}=9$ has the solution $k=2.$ Now, since ${3}^{k}$ increases as $k$ increases, the solution to ${3}^{k}<9$ must be $k<2.$"] [:db/cas 17592186332830 :question/problem "What is the sum of the series $1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+\\frac{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)^{2}}{2!}+⋯+\\frac{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)^{n}}{n!}+⋯\\,?$" "What is the sum of the series $$\n\\displaystyle 1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+\\frac{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)^{2}}{2!}+⋯+\\frac{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)^{n}}{n!}+⋯\\,?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332833 :question/problem "The power series ${\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{(x-2)}^{n}$ converges at  $x=0.$ Which of the following **must** be true?\n\nSelect all that apply." "The power series $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{(x-2)}^{n}\n$$ converges at  $x=0.$ Which of the following **must** be true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186332834 :question/problem "The power series ${\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{(x+2)}^{n}$ converges at  $x=-1.$ Which of the following **must** be true?\n\nSelect all that apply." "The power series $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{(x+2)}^{n}\n$$ converges at  $x=-1.$ Which of the following **must** be true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186332835 :question/problem "Given that the power series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}(x+1)^{n}$ converges at  $x=-4,$ which of the following **must** be true?\n\nSelect all that apply." "Given that the power series $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}(x+1)^{n}\n$$ converges at  $x=-4,$ which of the following **must** be true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186332836 :question/problem "The power series ${\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}(x+1)^{n}$ converges at  $x=\\frac{3}{2}.$ Which of the following **must** be true?\n\nSelect all that apply." "The power series $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}(x+1)^{n}\n$$ converges at  $x=\\frac{3}{2}.$ Which of the following **must** be true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186332836 :question/worked-solution "The center of the expansion is $x=-1,$ and since the series is convergent for $x=\\frac{3}{2},$ this means that the radius of convergence $R$ is *at least* $\\frac{5}{2},$ i.e., $R\\ge \\frac{5}{2}.$ So, the series is convergent for every point in the interval $(-\\frac{7}{2},\\frac{3}{2}].$ However, we know nothing about the convergence of the series at any point outside the interval $(-\\frac{7}{2},\\frac{3}{2}].$\n\n![](images/57/57208948dece1112674d3578763bc8d6b73d1d6ab91d160b14839e16a39cf2f6.png)\n\n \n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of our statements.\n\n- Statement “The series converges at $x=-3$” is true. Since $-3\\in (-\\frac{7}{2},\\frac{3}{2}],$ the series is convergent at $x=-3.$\n\n- Statement “The series converges at $x=-\\frac{7}{2}$” is false. Since $-\\frac{7}{2}\\notin (-\\frac{7}{2},\\frac{3}{2}],$ we cannot guarantee that the series is convergent at $x=-\\frac{7}{2}.$\n\n- Statement “The series converges at $x=2$” is false. Since $2\\notin (-\\frac{7}{2},\\frac{3}{2}],$ we cannot guarantee that the series is convergent at $x=2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “The series converges at $x=-3$” only." "The center of the expansion is $x=-1,$ and since the series is convergent for $x=\\frac{3}{2},$ this means that the radius of convergence $R$ is *at least* $\\frac{5}{2},$ i.e., $R\\ge \\frac{5}{2}.$ So, the series is convergent for every point in the interval $(-\\frac{7}{2},\\frac{3}{2}].$ However, we know nothing about the convergence of the series at any point outside the interval $(-\\frac{7}{2},\\frac{3}{2}].$\n\n![](images/57/57208948dece1112674d3578763bc8d6b73d1d6ab91d160b14839e16a39cf2f6.png)\n\n \n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of our statements.\n\n- Statement “The series converges at $x=-3$” is true. Since $-3\\in (-\\frac{7}{2},\\frac{3}{2}],$ the series is convergent at $x=-3.$\n\n- Statement “The series converges at $x=-\\frac{7}{2}$” is false. Since $$\n\\displaystyle -\\frac{7}{2}\\notin (-\\frac{7}{2},\\frac{3}{2}],\n$$ we cannot guarantee that the series is convergent at $x=-\\frac{7}{2}.$\n\n- Statement “The series converges at $x=2$” is false. Since $2\\notin (-\\frac{7}{2},\\frac{3}{2}],$ we cannot guarantee that the series is convergent at $x=2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “The series converges at $x=-3$” only."] [:db/cas 17592186332837 :question/problem "The power series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}(x-3)^{n}$ converges at  $x=-2.$ Which of the following **must** be true?\n\nSelect all that apply." "The power series $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}(x-3)^{n}\n$$ converges at  $x=-2.$ Which of the following **must** be true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186332840 :question/worked-solution "We use the flowchart below to determine which tests to use for each series.\n\n![](images/3a/3aa7f0243efeab51d00aa9694dd018245f7ca4664cc1a5fdf4e646aebb61536e.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's test each series in turn.\n\n- For the first series, we use the alternating series test. Observe that \n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{1}{n!}\n$$\n\nis positive and decreasing for all $n\\ge 1,$ and ${a}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty .$ So, by the alternating series test, the series\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{n!}\n$$\n\nis also convergent.\n\n- For the second series, we use the limit comparison test. Let \n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{2\\sqrt{n}}{n+2}\\,{b}_{n}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\nWorking out the necessary limit, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n}}{{b}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }(\\frac{2\\sqrt{n}}{n+2}÷\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{n}}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }(\\frac{2\\sqrt{n}}{n+2}\\cdot \\sqrt{n}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\left(\\frac{2n}{n+2}\\right) \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\left(\\frac{2}{(1+\\frac{2}{n})}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{1+0} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $L=2$ is positive and finite. Now, the series\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\nis divergent by the $p$-series test. So, the series \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2\\sqrt{n}}{n+2}\n$$\n\nis also divergent by the limit comparison test.\n\n- For the final series, we use the $n$th term test for divergence. Let \n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{{n}^{3}}{n+2{n}^{2}}\n$$\n\nThen, we have ${lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}=\\infty ,$ since the polynomial in the numerator grows faster than the polynomial in the denominator. Therefore, by the $n$th term test, the series\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{n}^{3}}{n+2{n}^{2}}\n$$\n\nis divergent.\n\nIn conclusion, only series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{n!}$ converges." "We use the flowchart below to determine which tests to use for each series.\n\n![](images/3a/3aa7f0243efeab51d00aa9694dd018245f7ca4664cc1a5fdf4e646aebb61536e.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's test each series in turn.\n\n- For the first series, we use the alternating series test. Observe that \n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{1}{n!}\n$$\n\nis positive and decreasing for all $n\\ge 1,$ and ${a}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty .$ So, by the alternating series test, the series\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{n!}\n$$\n\nis also convergent.\n\n- For the second series, we use the limit comparison test. Let \n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{2\\sqrt{n}}{n+2}\\,{b}_{n}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\nWorking out the necessary limit, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n}}{{b}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }(\\frac{2\\sqrt{n}}{n+2}÷\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{n}}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }(\\frac{2\\sqrt{n}}{n+2}\\cdot \\sqrt{n}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\left(\\frac{2n}{n+2}\\right) \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\left(\\frac{2}{(1+\\frac{2}{n})}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{1+0} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $L=2$ is positive and finite. Now, the series\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\nis divergent by the $p$-series test. So, the series \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2\\sqrt{n}}{n+2}\n$$\n\nis also divergent by the limit comparison test.\n\n- For the final series, we use the $n$th term test for divergence. Let \n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{{n}^{3}}{n+2{n}^{2}}\n$$\n\nThen, we have ${lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}=\\infty ,$ since the polynomial in the numerator grows faster than the polynomial in the denominator. Therefore, by the $n$th term test, the series\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{n}^{3}}{n+2{n}^{2}}\n$$\n\nis divergent.\n\nIn conclusion, only series $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{n!}\n$$ converges."] [:db/cas 17592186332848 :question/problem "${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332848 :question/worked-solution "We use the identity $\\operatorname{tan}⁡2x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this by substitution. Let $u=2x.$ Then $\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u.$ \n\nNow, we fill the table for the limits of integration, according to the rule $u=2x.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ |\n\nTherefore, the integral in terms of the new variable is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡u}{2}{|}_{0}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We use the identity $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{tan}⁡2x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x},\n$$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this by substitution. Let $u=2x.$ Then $\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u.$ \n\nNow, we fill the table for the limits of integration, according to the rule $u=2x.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ |\n\nTherefore, the integral in terms of the new variable is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡u}{2}{|}_{0}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332849 :question/problem "${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /24}({cos}^{2}⁡2x-{sin}^{2}⁡2x)\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /24}({cos}^{2}⁡2x-{sin}^{2}⁡2x)\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332860 :question/problem "$\\int {e}^{z}{e}^{z}\\,\\text{d}z=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int {e}^{z}{e}^{z}\\,\\text{d}z=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332862 :question/problem "Calculate $F(2)$ where $F(x)={\\int }_{0}^{x}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t.$" "Calculate $F(2)$ where $$\n\\displaystyle F(x)={\\int }_{0}^{x}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332883 :question/problem "![](images/7a/7aff74d99be667ea7b51bfcfaee2c0e701e3a553656aa931ba3f1f1ba0115ffb.png)\n\nConsider the graph of the function $f(x),$ shown above. It consists of two line segments. Find the value of ${\\int }_{0}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" "![](images/7a/7aff74d99be667ea7b51bfcfaee2c0e701e3a553656aa931ba3f1f1ba0115ffb.png)\n\nConsider the graph of the function $f(x),$ shown above. It consists of two line segments. Find the value of $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332896 :question/problem "What is the amplitude of the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{1}{3})?$" "What is the amplitude of the function $$\n\\displaystyle f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{1}{3})?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332923 :question/problem "Which of the following statements is true regarding the series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }7{\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{n}?$" "Which of the following statements is true regarding the series $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }7{\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{n}?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332939 :question/problem "Evaluate $(\\begin{aligned}15 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}).$" "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle (\\begin{aligned}15 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332945 :question/problem "${\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }-\\frac{6}{\\sqrt[5]{x}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }-\\frac{6}{\\sqrt[5]{x}}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332953 :question/problem "The length of a curve $y=f(x)$ is given by ${\\int }_{3}^{5}\\sqrt{1+25{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Which of the following could be  an equation for this curve?" "The length of a curve $y=f(x)$ is given by $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{3}^{5}\\sqrt{1+25{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$ Which of the following could be  an equation for this curve?"] [:db/cas 17592186332958 :question/problem "The length of a curve $y=f(x)$ is given by ${\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Which of the following could be  an equation for this curve?" "The length of a curve $y=f(x)$ is given by $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$ Which of the following could be  an equation for this curve?"] [:db/cas 17592186332961 :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{6}^{\\infty }\\frac{\\text{d}z}{z}.$" "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{6}^{\\infty }\\frac{\\text{d}z}{z}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186332986 :question/problem "Find the degree of the homogeneous function $g(x,y)=\\frac{y}{3x-y}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{y}\\right).$" "Find the degree of the homogeneous function $$\n\\displaystyle g(x,y)=\\frac{y}{3x-y}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{y}\\right).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186335694 :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- The equation of motion for a particle of mass $m$ moving with simple harmonic motion (SHM) under the action of a spring with spring constant $k$ is given by\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-ky.\n$$\n\nRearranging the above equation gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{k}{m}y=0.\n$$\n\nComparing this to the SHM equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe see that\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{k}{m}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $k$ and $m$ gives\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{8}{32}=\\frac{1}{4}\\,⟹\\,\\omega =\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nSo the governing differential equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{4}y=0.\n$$\n\nHence, statement “The governing differential equation is ${y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{4}y=0$” is true.\n\n- The frequency $f$ (measured in ${s}^{-1}$) is given by\n\n$$\nf=\\frac{1}{T}=\\frac{\\omega }{2\\pi },\n$$\n\nwhere $T$ is the period. So in our case, we have\n\n$$\nf=\\frac{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}{2\\pi }=\\frac{1}{4\\pi }\\,.\n$$\n\nSo the frequency $f=\\frac{1}{4\\pi }\\,{\\text{s}}^{-1}.$\nHence, statement “The frequency $f$ of the oscillation is $f=\\frac{1}{4\\pi }\\,{\\text{s}}^{-1}$” is true.\n\n- The general solution for $y(t)$ can take one of three forms.\n\n- $A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t$\n\n- $R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+{ϕ}_{1})$\n\n- $R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+{ϕ}_{2})$\n\nWriting $\\omega =\\frac{1}{2}$ in form 1 gives\n\n$$\ny(t)=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{t}{2}+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{t}{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, statement “The general solution can be expressed as $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{t}{4}+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{t}{4}$ where $R$ is the amplitude and $ϕ$ is the phase” is false.\n\nSo, the correct answer is “The governing differential equation is ${y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{4}y=0$”; “The frequency $f$ of the oscillation is $f=\\frac{1}{4\\pi }\\,{\\text{s}}^{-1}$” only." "Let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- The equation of motion for a particle of mass $m$ moving with simple harmonic motion (SHM) under the action of a spring with spring constant $k$ is given by\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-ky.\n$$\n\nRearranging the above equation gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{k}{m}y=0.\n$$\n\nComparing this to the SHM equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe see that\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{k}{m}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $k$ and $m$ gives\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{8}{32}=\\frac{1}{4}\\,⟹\\,\\omega =\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nSo the governing differential equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{4}y=0.\n$$\n\nHence, statement “The governing differential equation is ${y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{4}y=0$” is true.\n\n- The frequency $f$ (measured in ${s}^{-1}$) is given by\n\n$$\nf=\\frac{1}{T}=\\frac{\\omega }{2\\pi },\n$$\n\nwhere $T$ is the period. So in our case, we have\n\n$$\nf=\\frac{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}{2\\pi }=\\frac{1}{4\\pi }\\,.\n$$\n\nSo the frequency $f=\\frac{1}{4\\pi }\\,{\\text{s}}^{-1}.$\nHence, statement “The frequency $f$ of the oscillation is $f=\\frac{1}{4\\pi }\\,{\\text{s}}^{-1}$” is true.\n\n- The general solution for $y(t)$ can take one of three forms.\n\n- $A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t$\n\n- $R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+{ϕ}_{1})$\n\n- $R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+{ϕ}_{2})$\n\nWriting $\\omega =\\frac{1}{2}$ in form 1 gives\n\n$$\ny(t)=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{t}{2}+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{t}{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, statement “The general solution can be expressed as $$\n\\displaystyle y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{t}{4}+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{t}{4}\n$$ where $R$ is the amplitude and $ϕ$ is the phase” is false.\n\nSo, the correct answer is “The governing differential equation is ${y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{4}y=0$”; “The frequency $f$ of the oscillation is $f=\\frac{1}{4\\pi }\\,{\\text{s}}^{-1}$” only."] [:db/cas 17592186335710 :question/problem "Let $y={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}$ be a series solution to the equation $3{y}^{′}-y=0.$ \n\nWhat is the recurrence relation for the coefficients ${a}_{n},$ where $n=0,1,2,…?$" "Let $$\n\\displaystyle y={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}\n$$ be a series solution to the equation $3{y}^{′}-y=0.$ \n\nWhat is the recurrence relation for the coefficients ${a}_{n},$ where $n=0,1,2,…?$"] [:db/cas 17592186335711 :question/problem "Let $y={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}$ be a series solution to the equation $3{y}^{″}+y=0.$  Find $f(n),$ given that the recurrence relation for ${a}_{n}$ can be written as\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+2}=\\frac{{a}_{n}}{f(n)},\\,n=0,1,2,…\n$$" "Let $$\n\\displaystyle y={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}\n$$ be a series solution to the equation $3{y}^{″}+y=0.$  Find $f(n),$ given that the recurrence relation for ${a}_{n}$ can be written as\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+2}=\\frac{{a}_{n}}{f(n)},\\,n=0,1,2,…\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186335712 :question/problem "Let $y={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}$ be a series solution to the equation ${y}^{′}+7xy=0.$\n\nWhat is the recurrence relation for the coefficients ${a}_{n},$ where $n=1,2,3,…$" "Let $$\n\\displaystyle y={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}\n$$ be a series solution to the equation ${y}^{′}+7xy=0.$\n\nWhat is the recurrence relation for the coefficients ${a}_{n},$ where $n=1,2,3,…$"] [:db/cas 17592186335738 :question/worked-solution "Any point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{2}{4}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{2}{4}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. Therefore $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$\n\nUsing the fact that $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta },$ we finally get\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)}=1.\n$$" "Any point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{2}{4}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{2}{4}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. Therefore $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$\n\nUsing the fact that $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta },\n$$ we finally get\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)}=1.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186335763 :question/worked-solution "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/db/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{5\\pi }{4}$ corresponds to the point $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}).$\n\nSince the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\right)=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$" "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/db/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{5\\pi }{4}$ corresponds to the point $$\n\\displaystyle (-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}).\n$$\n\nSince the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\right)=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186335764 :question/worked-solution "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/db/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{11\\pi }{6}$ corresponds to the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},-\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\nSince the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $cos⁡\\left(\\frac{11\\pi }{6}\\right)=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.$" "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/db/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{11\\pi }{6}$ corresponds to the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},-\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\nSince the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $$\n\\displaystyle cos⁡\\left(\\frac{11\\pi }{6}\\right)=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186335780 :question/problem "Given that $D={x}^{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x},$ what is $D(y)$ for $y=2{x}^{3}+4?$" "Given that $$\n\\displaystyle D={x}^{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x},\n$$ what is $D(y)$ for $y=2{x}^{3}+4?$"] [:db/cas 17592186335781 :question/problem "Given that $D=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}+4\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+4\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x},$ what is $D(y)$ for $y={e}^{-2x}?$" "Given that $$\n\\displaystyle D=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}+4\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+4\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x},\n$$ what is $D(y)$ for $y={e}^{-2x}?$"] [:db/cas 17592186335813 :question/problem "If $\\frac{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}=\\frac{1}{f(x)},$ then $f(x)=$" "If $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}=\\frac{1}{f(x)},\n$$ then $f(x)=$"] [:db/cas 17592186335814 :question/problem "$\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }-\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }-\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186335818 :question/problem "Simplify the trigonometric expression $\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{3}⁡t}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}.$" "Simplify the trigonometric expression $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{3}⁡t}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186335819 :question/problem "Simplify the trigonometric expression $\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{3}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{3}⁡\\theta }.$" "Simplify the trigonometric expression $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{3}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{3}⁡\\theta }.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186335820 :question/problem "Simplify the trigonometric expression $\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{{cos}^{4}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }.$" "Simplify the trigonometric expression $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{{cos}^{4}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186335821 :question/problem "$\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186335823 :question/problem "If $\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+1}{{sin}^{2}⁡x}=\\frac{1}{f(x)},$ then $f(x)=$" "If $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+1}{{sin}^{2}⁡x}=\\frac{1}{f(x)},\n$$ then $f(x)=$"] [:db/cas 17592186335833 :question/problem "Simplify $\\frac{1+\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\alpha }{{tan}^{2}⁡\\alpha +\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\alpha +1}.$" "Simplify $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{1+\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\alpha }{{tan}^{2}⁡\\alpha +\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\alpha +1}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186335835 :question/problem "What is the exact value of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{11\\pi }{12}\\right)?$\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $\\frac{11\\pi }{12}=\\frac{7\\pi }{6}-\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$*" "What is the exact value of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{11\\pi }{12}\\right)?$\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{11\\pi }{12}=\\frac{7\\pi }{6}-\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$*"] [:db/cas 17592186335838 :question/problem "Simplify $\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta }-\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta .$" "Simplify $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta }-\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta .\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186335841 :question/problem "Calculate ${lim}_{x\\to \\pi }{csc}^{2}⁡(\\frac{x}{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}).$" "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle {lim}_{x\\to \\pi }{csc}^{2}⁡(\\frac{x}{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186335844 :question/problem "Simplify $\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta -1}.$" "Simplify $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta -1}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186335845 :question/worked-solution "The parameter $C$ represents a horizontal shift of the function.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the function by factoring the horizontal stretch factor, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{3x}{5}+C) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{3}{5}(x+\\frac{5C}{3}))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe factor $x+\\frac{5C}{3}$ indicates that the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{3}{5}(x+\\frac{5C}{3}))$ is a horizontal translation of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{3x}{5}\\right).$ Comparing the two graphs below allows us to determine the amount of this shift.\n\n![](images/5e/5e9b6568ec493d95db6fe72a6a19024326c5b920629e9ff44a85db54089aa2a6.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that our curve has been obtained by shifting $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{3x}{5}\\right)$ by $\\frac{\\pi }{3}-0=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ units to the right. Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\frac{5C}{3}=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\,⟹\\,C=\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right)\\,⟹\\,C=-\\frac{\\pi }{5}\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<C<\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $-\\frac{\\pi }{5}$ is in this interval, the value of $C$ is $-\\frac{\\pi }{5}.$" "The parameter $C$ represents a horizontal shift of the function.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the function by factoring the horizontal stretch factor, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{3x}{5}+C) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{3}{5}(x+\\frac{5C}{3}))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe factor $x+\\frac{5C}{3}$ indicates that the graph of $$\n\\displaystyle y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{3}{5}(x+\\frac{5C}{3}))\n$$ is a horizontal translation of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{3x}{5}\\right).$ Comparing the two graphs below allows us to determine the amount of this shift.\n\n![](images/5e/5e9b6568ec493d95db6fe72a6a19024326c5b920629e9ff44a85db54089aa2a6.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that our curve has been obtained by shifting $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{3x}{5}\\right)$ by $\\frac{\\pi }{3}-0=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ units to the right. Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\frac{5C}{3}=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\,⟹\\,C=\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right)\\,⟹\\,C=-\\frac{\\pi }{5}\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<C<\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $-\\frac{\\pi }{5}$ is in this interval, the value of $C$ is $-\\frac{\\pi }{5}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186335849 :question/problem "$\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\right)\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{10}\\right)=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\right)\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{10}\\right)=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186335873 :question/problem "Calculate the period of the function $y=-\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{x}{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$" "Calculate the period of the function $$\n\\displaystyle y=-\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{x}{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{2}).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186335873 :question/worked-solution "Given a function of the form $y=A\\operatorname{sec}⁡(Bx+C)+D,$ the period is $\\frac{2\\pi }{B}.$\n\nIn our case, we have that $B=\\frac{1}{3}.$ Therefore, the period is $\\frac{2\\pi }{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}=6\\pi .$" "Given a function of the form $y=A\\operatorname{sec}⁡(Bx+C)+D,$ the period is $\\frac{2\\pi }{B}.$\n\nIn our case, we have that $B=\\frac{1}{3}.$ Therefore, the period is $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{2\\pi }{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}=6\\pi .\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186335874 :question/problem "Which of the following expressions gives all of the vertical asymptotes of the function $f(x)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{3})?$ Assume that $n$ is an integer." "Which of the following expressions gives all of the vertical asymptotes of the function $$\n\\displaystyle f(x)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{3})?\n$$ Assume that $n$ is an integer."] [:db/cas 17592186335874 :question/worked-solution "The asymptotes of $y=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ coincide with the zeros of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ and are given by the following expression\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the asymptotes of $\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}),$ we replace the $x$ on the left-hand side of the above with $(\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}).$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi .\n$$\n\nSolving for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{3} & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{x}{2} & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\ \\frac{x}{2} & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}+n\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{2\\cdot \\pi }{6}+2n\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the expression giving all the vertical asymptotes is $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi ,$ where $n$ is an integer." "The asymptotes of $y=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ coincide with the zeros of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ and are given by the following expression\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the asymptotes of $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}),\n$$ we replace the $x$ on the left-hand side of the above with $(\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}).$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi .\n$$\n\nSolving for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{3} & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{x}{2} & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\ \\frac{x}{2} & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}+n\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{2\\cdot \\pi }{6}+2n\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the expression giving all the vertical asymptotes is $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi ,$ where $n$ is an integer."] [:db/cas 17592186335877 :question/problem "Find the range of the function $f(x)=\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})-1.$" "Find the range of the function $$\n\\displaystyle f(x)=\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})-1.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186335877 :question/worked-solution "The range of $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ consists of two intervals:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty )\n$$\n\nHorizontal shifts and stretches have no effect on the range. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nWe can split this into two inequalities:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\le -1,\\,\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\ge 1\n$$\n\nTo find the range of $f(x),$ we consider both intervals separately.\n\n- Multiplying the first interval by $\\frac{2}{3}$ and subtracting $1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\operatorname{sec}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\le -1 \\\\  & \\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\le -\\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & \\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})-1\\le -\\frac{2}{3}-1 \\\\  & f(x)\\le -\\frac{5}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Multiplying the second interval by $\\frac{2}{3}$ and subtracting $1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\operatorname{sec}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\ge 1 \\\\  & \\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\ge \\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & \\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})-1\\ge \\frac{2}{3}-1 \\\\  & f(x)\\ge -\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCombining both inequalities, we get\n\n$$\nf(x)\\le -\\frac{5}{3},\\,f(x)\\ge -\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the range is $f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,-\\frac{5}{3}]\\cup [-\\frac{1}{3},\\infty ).$" "The range of $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ consists of two intervals:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty )\n$$\n\nHorizontal shifts and stretches have no effect on the range. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nWe can split this into two inequalities:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\le -1,\\,\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\ge 1\n$$\n\nTo find the range of $f(x),$ we consider both intervals separately.\n\n- Multiplying the first interval by $\\frac{2}{3}$ and subtracting $1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\operatorname{sec}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\le -1 \\\\  & \\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\le -\\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & \\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})-1\\le -\\frac{2}{3}-1 \\\\  & f(x)\\le -\\frac{5}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Multiplying the second interval by $\\frac{2}{3}$ and subtracting $1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\operatorname{sec}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\ge 1 \\\\  & \\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\ge \\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & \\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})-1\\ge \\frac{2}{3}-1 \\\\  & f(x)\\ge -\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCombining both inequalities, we get\n\n$$\nf(x)\\le -\\frac{5}{3},\\,f(x)\\ge -\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the range is $$\n\\displaystyle f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,-\\frac{5}{3}]\\cup [-\\frac{1}{3},\\infty ).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186335878 :question/problem "What is the domain of the function $f(x)=-\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{2x}{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6})?$ Assume that $n$ is an integer." "What is the domain of the function $$\n\\displaystyle f(x)=-\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{2x}{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6})?\n$$ Assume that $n$ is an integer."] [:db/cas 17592186335878 :question/worked-solution "The domain of $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ consists of all values where $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\ne 0,$ and is given by the expression\n\n$$\nx\\ne n\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the domain of $-\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{2x}{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}),$ we replace the $x$ on the left-hand side of the above with $(\\frac{2x}{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}).$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{2x}{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\ne n\\pi .\n$$\n\nSolving for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x}{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6} & \\ne n\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{2x}{3} & \\ne \\frac{\\pi }{6}+n\\pi  \\\\ 2x & \\ne \\frac{3\\pi }{6}+3n\\pi  \\\\ 2x & \\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+3n\\pi  \\\\ x & \\ne \\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{3n\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of the function is $x\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{4}+3n\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ where $n$ is an integer." "The domain of $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ consists of all values where $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\ne 0,$ and is given by the expression\n\n$$\nx\\ne n\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the domain of $$\n\\displaystyle -\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{2x}{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}),\n$$ we replace the $x$ on the left-hand side of the above with $(\\frac{2x}{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}).$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{2x}{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\ne n\\pi .\n$$\n\nSolving for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x}{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6} & \\ne n\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{2x}{3} & \\ne \\frac{\\pi }{6}+n\\pi  \\\\ 2x & \\ne \\frac{3\\pi }{6}+3n\\pi  \\\\ 2x & \\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+3n\\pi  \\\\ x & \\ne \\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{3n\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of the function is $x\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{4}+3n\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ where $n$ is an integer."] [:db/cas 17592186335880 :question/problem "What is the domain of the function $f(x)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{x}{5}+\\frac{\\pi }{3})-1?$ Assume that $n$ is an integer." "What is the domain of the function $$\n\\displaystyle f(x)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{x}{5}+\\frac{\\pi }{3})-1?\n$$ Assume that $n$ is an integer."] [:db/cas 17592186335880 :question/worked-solution "The domain of $y=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ consists of all values where $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\ne 0,$ and is given by the expression\n\n$$\nx\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the domain of $\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{x}{5}+\\frac{\\pi }{3})-1,$ we replace the $x$ on the left-hand side of the above with $(\\frac{x}{5}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}).$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{5}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi .\n$$\n\nSolving for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{5}+\\frac{\\pi }{3} & \\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{x}{5} & \\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\ \\frac{x}{5} & \\ne \\frac{\\pi }{6}+n\\pi  \\\\ x & \\ne \\frac{5\\pi }{6}+5n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of the function is $x\\ne \\frac{5\\pi }{6}+5n\\pi ,$ where $n$ is an integer." "The domain of $y=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ consists of all values where $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\ne 0,$ and is given by the expression\n\n$$\nx\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the domain of $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{x}{5}+\\frac{\\pi }{3})-1,\n$$ we replace the $x$ on the left-hand side of the above with $(\\frac{x}{5}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}).$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{5}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi .\n$$\n\nSolving for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{5}+\\frac{\\pi }{3} & \\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{x}{5} & \\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\ \\frac{x}{5} & \\ne \\frac{\\pi }{6}+n\\pi  \\\\ x & \\ne \\frac{5\\pi }{6}+5n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of the function is $x\\ne \\frac{5\\pi }{6}+5n\\pi ,$ where $n$ is an integer."] [:db/cas 17592186335927 :question/problem "Which of the following statements is true regarding the series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{6}{(-1)}^{n}$ ?" "Which of the following statements is true regarding the series $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{6}{(-1)}^{n}\n$$ ?"] [:db/cas 17592186335929 :question/problem "Which of the following statements is true regarding the series  ${\\sum }_{n=3}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{5}{\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}^{n+1}$ ?" "Which of the following statements is true regarding the series  $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=3}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{5}{\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}^{n+1}\n$$ ?"] [:db/cas 17592186335930 :question/problem "Which of the following statements is true regarding the series \n ${\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(-4){\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)}^{2n}$ ?" "Which of the following statements is true regarding the series \n $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(-4){\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)}^{2n}\n$$ ?"] [:db/cas 17592186335976 :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{7\\,\\text{d}x}{|7x|\\sqrt{(7x)^{2}-1}}=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{7\\,\\text{d}x}{|7x|\\sqrt{(7x)^{2}-1}}=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186335977 :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{3\\,\\text{d}x}{|9x|\\sqrt{(9x)^{2}-1}}=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{3\\,\\text{d}x}{|9x|\\sqrt{(9x)^{2}-1}}=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186335982 :question/problem "What are the dimensions of the matrix $K$, where\n$K=\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}?$" "What are the dimensions of the matrix $K$, where\n$$\n\\displaystyle K=\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186335984 :question/problem "$\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -8 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 6 & -9\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 & -10 \\\\ -1 & 8 & -4\\end{bmatrix}=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}4 & -8 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 6 & -9\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 & -10 \\\\ -1 & 8 & -4\\end{bmatrix}=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186335985 :question/problem "$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 10\\end{bmatrix}=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 10\\end{bmatrix}=\n$$"] [:db